\h Acts
\toc1 Acts
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 ACT
\mt1 Acts
\c 1
\s1 Prologue
\r (\ref Luke 1:1–4|LUK 1:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 1 In my first book, O Theophilus, I wrote about all that Jesus began to do and to teach, \v 2 until the day He was taken up \add to heaven\add*, after giving instructions through \add the\add* Holy Spirit to the apostles He had chosen. \v 3 After His suffering, He presented Himself to \add them\add* with many convincing proofs \add that\add* He was alive. He appeared to them over \add a span \add* of forty days and spoke about the kingdom of God.
\p \v 4 And while they were gathered together,\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Or \fqa eating together\f* He commanded them: \wj “Do not leave Jerusalem, but wait for the \add gift\add* the Father promised, which you have heard Me \add discuss \add*.\wj* \v 5 \wj For John baptized with water, but in a few days you will be baptized with \add the\add* Holy Spirit.\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Or \fqa For John baptized in water, but in a few days you will be baptized in the Holy Spirit\ft ; cited in \ref Acts 11:16|ACT 11:16\ref*\f*”\wj*
\s1 The Ascension
\r (\ref Mark 16:19–20|MRK 16:19-20\ref*; \ref Luke 24:50–53|LUK 24:50-53\ref*)
\p \v 6 So \add when\add* they came together, they asked Him, “Lord, \add will You\add* at this time restore the kingdom to Israel?”
\p \v 7 \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “It is not for you to know times or seasons that the Father has fixed by His own authority.\wj* \v 8 \wj But you will receive power \add when\add* the Holy Spirit comes upon you, and you will be My witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to \add the\add* ends of the earth.”\wj*
\p \v 9 \add After\add* He had said this, they watched as He was taken up, and a cloud hid Him from their sight. \v 10 They were looking intently into the sky as He was going, \add when\add* suddenly two men dressed in white stood beside them. \v 11 “Men of Galilee,” they said, “why do you stand \add here\add* looking into the sky? This \add same\add* Jesus, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back in the same way you have seen Him go into heaven.”
\s1 Matthias Replaces Judas
\p \v 12 Then they returned to Jerusalem from \add the\add* Mount of Olives, which is near \add the city\add*, a Sabbath day’s journey away.\f + \fr 1:12 \fqa A Sabbath day’s journey \ft is 2,000 cubits (approximately 3,000 feet or 914.4 meters)\f* \v 13 When they arrived, they went to the upper room where they were staying: Peter and John, James and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James \add son\add* of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, and Judas \add son\add* of James. \v 14 With one accord they all continued in prayer, along with \add the\add* women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with His brothers.
\p \v 15 In those days Peter stood up among the brothers (a gathering of about a hundred \add and\add* twenty) \add and\add* said, \v 16 “Brothers, the Scripture had to be fulfilled that the Holy Spirit foretold through \add the\add* mouth of David concerning Judas, who became a guide for those who arrested Jesus. \v 17 He was one of our number and shared in this ministry.”
\p \v 18 (Now with \add the\add* reward for \add his\add* wickedness \add Judas\add* bought a field; \add there\add* he fell headlong and burst open in \add the\add* middle, and all his intestines spilled out. \v 19 \add This\add* became known to all who lived in Jerusalem, so they called that field in their own language Akeldama, that is, Field of Blood.)
\p \v 20 “For it is written in \add the\add* book of Psalms:
\b 
\q1 ‘\add May\add* his place be deserted;
\q2 let there be no \add one\add* to dwell in it,’\f + \fr 1:20 \ref Psalm 69:25|PSA 69:25\ref*\f*
\p and,
\b 
\q1 ‘\add May\add* another take his position.’\f + \fr 1:20 \ref Psalm 109:8|PSA 109:8\ref*\f*
\p \v 21 Therefore it is necessary \add to choose\add* one of the men who have accompanied us \add the\add* whole time the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, \v 22 beginning from John’s baptism until the day \add Jesus\add* was taken up from us. \add For\add* one of these must become a witness with us of His resurrection.”
\p \v 23 \add So\add* they proposed two \add men\add*: Joseph called Barsabbas (\add also\add* known as Justus) and Matthias. \v 24 And they prayed, “Lord, You know everyone’s heart. Show \add us\add* which of these two You have chosen \v 25 to take up this ministry and apostleship, which Judas abandoned to go to his \add rightful\add* place.”
\p \v 26 Then they cast lots, and the lot fell to Matthias. \add So\add* he was added to the eleven apostles.
\c 2
\s1 The Holy Spirit at Pentecost
\r (\ref Genesis 11:1–9|GEN 11:1-9\ref*; \ref Leviticus 23:15–22|LEV 23:15-22\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add When\add* the day of Pentecost\f + \fr 2:1 \ft That is, Shavuot, the late spring feast of pilgrimage to Jerusalem; it is also known as \fqa the Feast of Harvest\ft (see \ref Exodus 23:16|EXO 23:16\ref*) or \fqa the Feast of Weeks\ft (see \ref Exodus 34:22|EXO 34:22\ref*).\f* came, they were all together in one \add place\add*. \v 2 Suddenly a sound like a mighty rushing wind came from heaven and filled the whole house where they were sitting. \v 3 They saw tongues like flames of fire that separated and came to rest on each of them. \v 4 And they \add were\add* all filled with \add the\add* Holy Spirit and began to speak in other tongues as the Spirit enabled them.
\p \v 5 Now there were dwelling\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Or \fqa staying\ft ; similarly in verse 14\f* in Jerusalem God-fearing Jews from every nation under heaven. \v 6 And \add when\add* this sound rang out, a crowd came together in bewilderment, because each one heard them speaking \add his\add* own language.
\p \v 7 Astounded and amazed, they asked, “Are not all these who are speaking Galileans? \v 8 How is it then that each of us hears \add them\add* in his own native language? \v 9 Parthians, Medes, and Elamites; residents of Mesopotamia, Judea and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia,\f + \fr 2:9 \ft That is, the Roman Province of Asia, located in what is now western Turkey\f* \v 10 Phrygia and Pamphylia, Egypt and the parts of Libya near Cyrene; visitors from Rome, \v 11 both Jews and converts to Judaism; Cretans and Arabs— we hear them declaring the wonders of God \add in\add* our own tongues!”
\p \v 12 Astounded and perplexed, they asked one another, “What does this mean?”
\p \v 13 But others mocked \add them and\add* said, “They are drunk on new wine!”
\s1 Peter Addresses the Crowd
\r (\ref Psalm 16:1–11|PSA 16:1-11\ref*; \ref Joel 2:28–32|JOL 2:28-32\ref*)
\p \v 14 Then Peter stood up with the Eleven, lifted up his voice, and addressed \add the crowd\add*: “Men of Judea and all who dwell in Jerusalem, let this be known to you, and listen carefully to my words. \v 15 These \add men\add* are not drunk, as you suppose. It is \add only the\add* third hour of the day!\f + \fr 2:15 \ft That is, nine in the morning\f* \v 16 No, this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel:
\b 
\q1 \v 17 ‘In the last days, God says,
\q2 I will pour out My Spirit on all people.
\q1 Your sons and daughters will prophesy,
\q2 your young men will see visions,
\q2 your old men will dream dreams.
\q1 \v 18 Even on My menservants and maidservants
\q2 I will pour out My Spirit in those days,
\q2 and they will prophesy.
\q1 \v 19 I will show wonders in the heavens above
\q2 and signs on the earth below,
\q2 blood and fire and billows of smoke.
\q1 \v 20 The sun will be turned to darkness,
\q2 and the moon to blood,
\q2 before the coming \add of the\add* great and glorious Day of \add the\add* Lord.
\q1 \v 21 And everyone who calls on the name of \add the\add* Lord
\q2 will be saved.’\f + \fr 2:21 \ref Joel 2:28–32|JOL 2:28-32\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 22 Men of Israel, listen to this message: Jesus of Nazareth \add was\add* a man certified by God to you by miracles, wonders, and signs, which God did among you through Him, as you yourselves know. \v 23 He was delivered up by God’s set plan and foreknowledge, \add and you\add*, by the hands of the lawless, put \add Him\add* to death by nailing \add Him\add* to the cross. \v 24 \add But\add* God raised Him from the dead, releasing \add Him\add* from the agony of death, because it was impossible \add for death\add* to keep Him in its \add grip\add*.
\p \v 25 David says about Him:
\b 
\q1 ‘I saw\f + \fr 2:25 \ft Literally \fqa I foresaw\ft ; see verse 31.\f* the Lord always before me;
\q2 because He is at my right hand, I will not be shaken.
\q1 \v 26 Therefore my heart is glad and my tongue rejoices;
\q2 my body also will dwell in hope,
\q1 \v 27 because You will not abandon my soul to Hades,
\q2 nor will You let Your Holy One see decay.
\q1 \v 28 You have made known to me \add the\add* paths of life;
\q2 You will fill me with joy in Your presence.’\f + \fr 2:28 \ref Psalm 16:8–11|PSA 16:8-11\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 29 Brothers, I can tell you with confidence that the patriarch David died and was buried, and his tomb is with us to this day. \v 30 \add But\add* he was a prophet and knew that God had promised him on oath that He would place \add one\add* of \add his descendants\add* on his throne.\f + \fr 2:30 \ft Literally \fqa on oath that He would place out of the fruit of his loins on his throne\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa on oath out of the fruit of his loins, according to the flesh, to raise up Christ to sit on his throne\ft ; \ref Psalm 132:11|PSA 132:11\ref*\f* \v 31 Foreseeing this, \add David\add* spoke about the resurrection of the Christ, that He was not abandoned to Hades, nor \add did\add* His body see decay. \v 32 God has raised this Jesus \add to life\add*, to which we are all witnesses.
\p \v 33 Exalted, then, to the right hand of God, He has received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit \add and\add* has poured out what you now see and hear. \v 34 For David \add did\add* not ascend into heaven, but he himself says:
\b 
\q1 ‘The Lord said to my Lord,
\q2 “Sit at My right hand
\q1 \v 35 until I make Your enemies
\q2 a footstool for Your feet.”’\f + \fr 2:35 \ref Psalm 110:1|PSA 110:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 36 Therefore \add let\add* all Israel know with certainty that God has made this Jesus, whom you crucified, both Lord and Christ!”
\s1 Three Thousand Believe
\p \v 37 \add When the people\add* heard \add this\add*, they were cut to the heart and asked Peter and the other apostles, “Brothers, what shall we do?”
\p \v 38 Peter replied, “Repent and be baptized, every one of you, in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins, and you will receive the gift of the Holy Spirit. \v 39 \add This\add* promise belongs to you and your children and to all who \add are\add* far off — to all whom \add the\add* Lord our God will call to Himself.”
\p \v 40 With many other words he testified, and he urged them, “Be saved from this corrupt generation.” \v 41 Those who embraced his message were baptized, and about three thousand were added  \add to the believers \add* that day.\f + \fr 2:41 \ft Literally \fqa about three thousand souls were added that day\f*
\s1 The Fellowship of Believers
\r (\ref Acts 4:32–37|ACT 4:32-37\ref*)
\p \v 42 They devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and to the fellowship, to the breaking of bread and to prayer. \v 43 A sense of awe\f + \fr 2:43 \ft Or \fqa fear\f* came over everyone, and the apostles performed many wonders and signs.
\p \v 44 All the believers were together and had everything in common. \v 45 Selling \add their\add* possessions and goods, they shared with anyone who was in need.
\p \v 46 With one accord they continued \add to meet\add* daily in the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 2:46 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\f* and to break bread from house \add to house\add*, sharing \add their\add* meals with gladness and sincerity of heart, \v 47 praising God and enjoying \add the\add* favor of all the people. And the Lord added to their number daily those who were being saved.
\c 3
\s1 A Lame Man Walks
\p \v 1  \add One afternoon \add* Peter and John were going up to the temple at the hour of prayer, the ninth \add hour\add*.\f + \fr 3:1 \ft That is, three in the afternoon\f* \v 2 And a man who was lame from birth was being carried \add to\add* the temple gate called Beautiful, \add where\add* he was put every day to beg from those entering the temple \add courts\add*.\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verse 8\f* \v 3 \add When\add* he saw Peter and John about to enter, he asked \add them\add* for money.
\p \v 4 Peter looked directly at him, as \add did\add* John. “Look at us!” said \add Peter\add*. \v 5 \add So the man\add* gave them his attention, expecting to receive something from them. \v 6 But Peter said, “Silver \add or\add* gold I do not have, but what I have I give you: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, get up and walk!\f + \fr 3:6 \ft SBL, NE, and WH \fqa In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, walk!\f*”
\p \v 7 Taking him by the right hand, \add Peter\add* helped him up, and at once \add the man’s\add* feet and ankles were made strong. \v 8 He sprang to his feet and began to walk. Then he went with them into the temple \add courts\add*, walking and leaping and praising God.
\p \v 9 \add When\add* all the people saw him walking and praising God, \v 10 they recognized him \add as\add* the \add man\add* who used to sit \add begging\add* at the Beautiful Gate of the temple, and they were filled with wonder and amazement at what had happened to him.
\s1 Peter Speaks in Solomon’s Colonnade
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 18:15–22|DEU 18:15-22\ref*)
\p \v 11 \add While the man\add* clung to Peter and John, all the people were astonished \add and\add* ran to them in the \add walkway\add* called Solomon’s Colonnade.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Literally \fqa in the colonnade called Solomon’s\f* \v 12 And \add when\add* Peter saw \add this\add*, he addressed the people: “Men of Israel, why are you surprised by this? Why do you stare at us as if by \add our\add* own power or godliness \add we\add* had made \add this man\add* walk?
\p \v 13 The God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, the God of our fathers, has glorified His servant\f + \fr 3:13 \ft Or \fqa His child\ft ; also in verse 26\f* Jesus. You handed \add Him over\add* and rejected \add Him\add* before Pilate, \add even though\add* he had decided to release \add Him\add*. \v 14 You rejected the Holy and Righteous One and asked that a murderer be released to you. \v 15 You killed the Author of life, \add but\add* God raised \add Him\add* from \add the\add* dead, \add and\add* we are witnesses of this \add fact\add*.
\p \v 16 By faith in \add the\add* name of Jesus, this \add man\add* whom you see and know has been made strong. \add It is Jesus’\add* name and the faith that \add comes\add* through Him \add that\add* has given him this complete healing in your presence.
\p \v 17 And now, brothers, I know that you acted in ignorance, as \add did\add* your leaders. \v 18 But in this way \add God\add* has fulfilled what \add He\add* foretold through all the prophets, \add saying that\add* His Christ would suffer. \v 19 Repent, then, and turn back, so that your sins may be wiped away, \v 20 that times of refreshing may come from \add the\add* presence of the Lord, and \add that\add* He may send Jesus, \add the\add* Christ, who has been appointed for you.
\p \v 21 Heaven must take Him in until \add the\add* time \add comes\add* for \add the\add* restoration of all things, which God announced long ago through His holy prophets. \v 22 For Moses said, ‘The Lord your God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brothers. You must listen to Him in everything He tells you.\f + \fr 3:22 \ref Deuteronomy 18:15|DEU 18:15\ref*\f* \v 23 Everyone who does not listen  \add to Him \add* will be completely cut off from among \add his\add* people.\f + \fr 3:23 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 18:19|DEU 18:19\ref*.\f*’
\p \v 24 Indeed, all the prophets from Samuel on, as many as have spoken, have proclaimed these days. \v 25 \add And\add* you are sons of the prophets and of the covenant God made with your fathers \add when\add* He said to Abraham, ‘Through your offspring all the families of the earth will be blessed.’\f + \fr 3:25 \ref Genesis 22:18|GEN 22:18\ref*\f* \v 26 \add When\add* God raised up His Servant, He sent Him first to you to bless you by turning each \add of you\add* from your wicked ways.”
\c 4
\s1 Peter and John before the Sanhedrin
\p \v 1 While \add Peter and John\add* were speaking to the people, the priests and the captain of the temple guard and the Sadducees came up to them, \v 2 greatly disturbed that they were teaching the people and proclaiming in Jesus the resurrection of \add the\add* dead. \v 3 They seized \add Peter and John\add*, and because it was evening, they put \add them\add* in custody until the next day. \v 4 But many who heard the message believed, and the number of men grew to about five thousand.
\p \v 5 The next day \add the\add* rulers, elders, and scribes assembled in Jerusalem, \v 6 along with Annas the high priest, Caiaphas, John, Alexander, and many others from the high priest’s family. \v 7 They had \add Peter and John brought\add* in \add and\add* began to question them: “By what power or what name \add did\add* you do this?”
\p \v 8 Then Peter, filled with \add the\add* Holy Spirit, said to them, “Rulers and elders of the people! \v 9 If we are being examined today about a kind service \add to a\add* man \add who was\add* lame, \add to determine\add* how he was healed, \v 10 \add then\add* let this be known to all of you and to all the people of Israel: \add It is\add* by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom you crucified \add but\add* whom God raised from \add the\add* dead, \add that\add* this \add man\add* stands before you healed. \v 11 This \add Jesus\add* is
\b 
\q1 ‘the stone you builders rejected,
\q2 which has become \add the\add* cornerstone.’\f + \fr 4:11 \ref Psalm 118:22|PSA 118:22\ref*\f*
\p \v 12 Salvation exists in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given to men by which we must be saved.”
\s1 The Name Forbidden
\p \v 13 \add When\add* they saw the boldness of Peter and John and realized that they were unschooled, ordinary men, they marveled and took note that \add these men\add* had been with Jesus. \v 14 And seeing the man who had been healed standing \add there\add* with them, they had nothing to say in response. \v 15 So they ordered them to leave the Sanhedrin\f + \fr 4:15 \ft Or \fqa the Council\f* \add and\add* then conferred together.
\p \v 16 “What shall we do with these men?” they asked. “\add It\add* is clear to everyone living \add in\add* Jerusalem that a remarkable miracle has occurred through them, and we cannot deny \add it\add*. \v 17 But to keep \add this message\add* from spreading any further among the people, we must warn them not to speak to anyone in this name.”
\p \v 18 Then they called them in \add again\add* and commanded \add them\add* not to speak \add or\add* teach at all in the name of Jesus. \v 19 But Peter and John replied, “Judge for yourselves whether it is right in God’s sight to listen to you rather than God. \v 20 For we cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.”
\p \v 21 \add After\add* further threats they let them go. They could not find a way to punish them, because \add all\add* the people were glorifying God for what had happened. \v 22 For the man who was miraculously healed was over forty years \add old\add*.
\s1 The Believers’ Prayer
\r (\ref Psalm 2:1–12|PSA 2:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 23 On their release, \add Peter and John\add* returned to \add their\add* own people and reported everything that the chief priests and elders had said to them. \v 24 \add When the believers\add* heard this, they lifted up \add their\add* voices to God with one accord. “Sovereign Lord,” they said, “You made the heaven and the earth and the sea and everything in them. \v 25 \add You\add* spoke by \add the\add* Holy Spirit through \add the\add* mouth of Your servant,\f + \fr 4:25 \ft Or \fqa child\ft ; also in verses 27 and 30\f* our father David:
\b 
\q1 ‘Why \add do\add* the nations rage
\q2 and \add the\add* peoples plot in vain?
\q1 \v 26 The kings of the earth take \add their\add* stand
\q2 and the rulers gather together
\q1 against the Lord
\q2 and against His Anointed One.’\f + \fr 4:26 \ft Or \fqa His Christ \ft or \fqa His Messiah\ft ; \ref Psalm 2:1–2|PSA 2:1-2\ref*\f*
\p \v 27 In fact, this is the \add very\add* city \add where\add* Herod and Pontius Pilate conspired with \add the\add* Gentiles and \add the\add* people of Israel against Your holy servant Jesus, whom You anointed. \v 28 They carried out what Your hand and will had decided beforehand would happen. \v 29 And now, Lord, consider their threats, and enable Your servants to speak Your word with complete boldness, \v 30 as You stretch out Your hand to heal and \add perform\add* signs and wonders through the name of Your holy servant Jesus.”
\p \v 31 \add After\add* they had prayed, \add their\add* meeting place was shaken, and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke the word of God boldly.
\s1 Sharing among Believers
\r (\ref Acts 2:42–47|ACT 2:42-47\ref*)
\p \v 32 The multitude of believers was one \add in\add* heart and soul. No one claimed that any of his possessions was his own, but they shared everything they owned. \v 33 \add With\add* great power the apostles continued to give \add their\add* testimony about the resurrection of the Lord Jesus. And abundant grace was upon them all.
\p \v 34 There were no needy ones among them, because those who owned lands or houses would sell  \add their property \add*, bring the proceeds from \add the\add* sales, \v 35 and lay \add them\add* at the apostles’ feet for distribution \add to\add* anyone as he had need.
\p \v 36 Joseph, a Levite from Cyprus, whom the apostles called Barnabas (meaning Son of Encouragement), \v 37 sold a field he owned, brought the money, and laid \add it\add* at the apostles’ feet.
\c 5
\s1 Ananias and Sapphira
\p \v 1 Now a man named Ananias, together with his wife Sapphira, \add also\add* sold a piece of property. \v 2 \add With his\add* wife’s full knowledge, he kept back \add some\add* of the proceeds \add for himself\add*, \add but\add* brought a portion \add and\add* laid \add it\add* at the apostles’ feet.
\p \v 3 Then Peter said, “Ananias, how is it that Satan has filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit and withhold \add some\add* of the proceeds from the land? \v 4 Did it not belong to you before it was sold? And after it was sold, was it \add not\add* at \add your\add* disposal? How could you conceive such a deed in your heart? You have not lied to men, but to God!”
\p \v 5 On hearing these words, Ananias fell down \add and\add* died. And great fear came over all who heard \add what had happened\add*. \v 6 Then the young \add men\add* stepped forward, wrapped up his \add body\add*, and carried \add him\add* out \add and\add* buried \add him\add*.
\p \v 7 About three hours later his wife also came in, unaware of what had happened. \v 8 “Tell me,” said Peter, “\add is this\add* the price you \add and your husband \add* got for the land?”
\p “Yes,” she answered, “that is the price.”
\p \v 9 “How could you agree to test the Spirit of \add the\add* Lord?” Peter \add replied\add*. “Look, the feet of the \add men who\add* buried your husband \add are\add* at the door, and they will carry you out \add also\add*.”
\p \v 10 At that instant she fell down at his feet and died. Then the young \add men\add* came in \add and\add*, finding her dead, carried \add her\add* out \add and\add* buried \add her\add* beside her husband. \v 11 And great fear came over the whole church and all who heard about these \add events\add*.
\s1 The Apostles Heal Many
\p \v 12 The apostles performed many signs and wonders among the people, and with one accord \add the believers gathered together\add* in Solomon’s Colonnade. \v 13 Although the people regarded them highly, no one else dared to join them. \v 14 Yet more and more believers were brought to the Lord — large numbers of both men and women.
\p \v 15 As a result, \add people\add* brought the sick into the streets and laid \add them\add* on cots and mats, so that at least Peter’s shadow might fall on some of them as he passed by. \v 16 Crowds also gathered from the towns around Jerusalem, bringing \add the\add* sick and \add those\add* tormented by unclean spirits, \add and\add* all \add of them\add* were healed.
\s1 The Apostles Arrested and Freed
\p \v 17 Then the high priest and all his associates, who belonged to the party of the Sadducees, were filled with jealousy. They went out \v 18 and arrested the apostles and put them in \add the\add* public jail. \v 19 But during the night an angel of \add the\add* Lord opened the doors of the jail \add and\add* brought them out, saying, \v 20 “Go, stand in the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 5:20 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verses 21, 25, and 42\f* \add and\add* tell the people the full message of this \add new\add* life.”
\p \v 21 At daybreak \add the apostles\add* entered the temple \add courts\add* as they had been told and began to teach \add the people\add*.
\p \add When\add* the high priest and his associates arrived, they convened the Sanhedrin\f + \fr 5:21 \ft Or \fqa the Council\ft ; also in verses 27, 34, and 41\f*— the full assembly of the elders of Israel— and sent to the jail \add for the apostles\add*. \v 22 But on arriving at the jail, the officers did not find them \add there\add*. \add So\add* they returned with the report: \v 23 “We found the jail securely locked, \add with\add* the guards posted at the doors; but when we opened \add them\add*, we found no one inside.”
\s1 The Apostles before the Sanhedrin
\p \v 24 When the captain of the temple \add guard\add* and the chief priests heard this account, they were perplexed as to what was happening.\f + \fr 5:24 \ft Literally \fqa as to what this might be\f* \v 25 Then someone came in \add and\add* announced, “Look, the men you put in jail are standing in the temple \add courts\add* teaching the people!”
\p \v 26 At that point, the captain went with the officers \add and\add* brought \add the apostles\add* — \add but\add* not by force, for fear the people would stone \add them\add*. \v 27 They brought them in \add and\add* made \add them\add* stand before the Sanhedrin, \add where\add* the high priest interrogated them. \v 28 “We gave you strict orders not to teach in this name,” he said. “Yet you have filled Jerusalem with your teaching and are determined to make us responsible for this man’s blood.”
\p \v 29 But Peter and the other apostles replied, “We must obey God rather than men. \v 30 The God of our fathers raised up Jesus, whom you had killed by hanging \add Him\add* on a tree. \v 31 God exalted Him to His right hand \add as\add* Prince and Savior, \add in order \add* to grant repentance and forgiveness of sins to Israel. \v 32 We are witnesses of these things, and so \add is\add* the Holy Spirit, whom God has given to those who obey Him.”
\s1 Gamaliel’s Advice
\p \v 33 When \add the Council members\add* heard this, they were enraged, and they resolved\f + \fr 5:33 \ft ECM, BYZ, TR, and Tischendorf; or \fqa they took counsel\ft ; SBL, NE, and WH \fqa they wanted\f* to put \add the apostles\add* to death. \v 34 But a Pharisee named Gamaliel, a teacher of the law who was honored by all the people, stood up in the Sanhedrin \add and\add* ordered that the men be put outside for a short time.
\p \v 35 “Men of Israel,” he said, “consider carefully what you are about to do to these men. \v 36 Some time ago Theudas rose up, claiming to be somebody, \add and\add* about four hundred men joined \add him\add*. \add He\add* was killed, all his followers were dispersed, and it all came to nothing. \v 37 After \add him\add*, Judas the Galilean appeared in the days of the census and drew away people after him. He too perished, and all his followers were scattered.
\p \v 38 So in the present \add case\add* I advise you: Leave these men \add alone\add*. Let them go! For if \add their\add* purpose or endeavor is of human origin, it will fail. \v 39 But if it is from God, you will not be able to stop them. You may even find yourselves fighting against God.”
\p \v 40 At this, they yielded to \add Gamaliel\add*. They called the apostles \add in\add* and had them flogged. Then they ordered \add them\add* not to speak in the name of Jesus, and released \add them\add*.
\p \v 41 \add The apostles\add* left the Sanhedrin, rejoicing that they had been counted worthy of suffering disgrace for the Name. \v 42 Every day, in the temple \add courts\add* and from house \add to house\add*, they did not stop teaching and proclaiming the good news that Jesus \add is\add* the Christ.
\c 6
\s1 The Choosing of the Seven
\r (\ref 1 Timothy 3:8–13|1TI 3:8-13\ref*)
\p \v 1 In those days \add when\add* the disciples were increasing in number, the Grecian \add Jews among them\add* began to grumble against the Hebraic \add Jews\add*\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Literally \fqa the Hellenists began to grumble against the Hebrews\f* because their widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution  \add of food \add*.
\p \v 2 So the Twelve summoned all the disciples \add and\add* said, “It is unacceptable \add for\add* us to neglect the word of God \add in order\add* to wait on tables. \v 3 Therefore, brothers, select from among you seven men confirmed to be full of \add the\add* Spirit and wisdom. We will assign this responsibility \add to them\add* \v 4 and will devote ourselves to prayer and to the ministry of the word.”
\p \v 5 \add This\add* proposal pleased the whole group. They chose Stephen, a man full of faith and \add of the\add* Holy Spirit, as well as Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas, and Nicolas from Antioch, a convert \add to Judaism\add*.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft Literally \fqa and Nicolas, a convert of Antioch\f* \v 6 They presented \add these seven\add* to the apostles,\f + \fr 6:6 \ft Literally \fqa —whom they set before the apostles,\f* \add who\add* prayed \add and\add* laid \add their\add* hands on them.
\p \v 7 \add So\add* the word of God continued to spread. The number of disciples in Jerusalem grew rapidly, and a great number of priests became obedient to the faith.
\s1 The Arrest of Stephen
\p \v 8 Now Stephen, \add who was\add* full of grace and power, was performing great wonders and signs among the people. \v 9 But resistance arose from what was called \add the\add* Synagogue of the Freedmen, including Cyrenians, Alexandrians, and men from \add the provinces of\add* Cilicia and Asia.\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Literally \fqa and those from Cilicia and Asia\ft ; the Roman Province of Asia was located in what is now western Turkey.\f* They disputed with Stephen, \v 10 \add but\add* they could not stand up to \add his\add* wisdom \add or\add* the Spirit by whom he spoke.
\p \v 11 Then they prompted \add some\add* men to say, “We heard \add Stephen\add* speak words of blasphemy against Moses and \add against\add* God.”
\p \v 12 \add So\add* they stirred up the people, elders, and scribes and confronted \add Stephen\add*. They seized him and brought \add him\add* before the Sanhedrin,\f + \fr 6:12 \ft Or \fqa the Council\ft ; also in verse 15\f* \v 13 \add where\add* they presented false witnesses \add who\add* said, “This man never stops speaking against this holy place and \add against\add* the law. \v 14 For we have heard him say that Jesus of Nazareth will destroy this place and change the customs that Moses handed down to us.”
\p \v 15 All who were sitting in the Sanhedrin looked intently at \add Stephen\add*, \add and\add* they saw that his face \add was\add* like \add the\add* face of an angel.
\c 7
\s1 Stephen’s Address: The Call of Abraham
\r (\ref Genesis 12:1–9|GEN 12:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then the high priest asked \add Stephen\add*, “Are these \add charges\add* true?”
\p \v 2 And \add Stephen\add* declared: “Brothers and fathers, listen \add to me\add*! The God of glory appeared to our father Abraham while he was \add still\add* in Mesopotamia, before he lived in Haran, \v 3 and told him, ‘Leave your country and your kindred and go to the land I will show you.’\f + \fr 7:3 \ref Genesis 12:1|GEN 12:1\ref*\f* \v 4 \add So Abraham\add* left \add the\add* land of the Chaldeans \add and\add* settled in Haran. After his father died, \add God\add* brought him out of that place \add and\add* into this land where you \add are\add* now living.
\p \v 5 He gave him no inheritance \add here\add*, not even a foot of ground. \add But God\add* promised to give possession of \add the land\add* to \add Abraham\add* and his descendants, \add even though\add* he did not yet have a child. \v 6 God told \add him\add* that his descendants would be foreigners in a strange land, and \add that\add* they would be enslaved and mistreated four hundred years. \v 7 ‘\add But\add* I will punish the nation \add that\add* enslaves them,’ God said, ‘and afterward they will come forth and worship Me in this place.’\f + \fr 7:7 \ref Genesis 15:13–14|GEN 15:13-14\ref*; Exodus 3:12\f*
\p \v 8 Then \add God\add* gave \add Abraham the\add* covenant of circumcision, and \add Abraham\add* became the father of Isaac and circumcised him on the eighth day. And Isaac \add became the father of\add* Jacob, and Jacob of the twelve patriarchs.
\s1 Joseph Sold into Egypt
\r (\ref Genesis 37:12–30|GEN 37:12-30\ref*)
\p \v 9 \add Because\add* the patriarchs were jealous of Joseph, they sold him as a slave into Egypt. But God was with him \v 10 and rescued him from all his troubles. \add He\add* granted \add Joseph\add* favor and wisdom in the sight of Pharaoh king of Egypt, \add who\add* appointed him ruler over Egypt and all his household.
\p \v 11 Then famine and great suffering swept across Egypt and Canaan, and our fathers could not find food. \v 12 \add When\add* Jacob heard that \add there\add* was grain in Egypt, he sent our fathers \add on their\add* first \add visit\add*. \v 13 On \add their\add* second \add visit\add*, Joseph revealed his identity to\f + \fr 7:13 \ft Or \fqa Joseph was made known to \ft or \fqa Joseph was recognized by\f* his brothers, and \add his\add* family became known to Pharaoh. \v 14 Then Joseph sent for his father Jacob and all \add his\add* relatives, seventy-five in all.
\s1 Israel Oppressed in Egypt
\r (\ref Exodus 1:8–22|EXO 1:8-22\ref*)
\p \v 15 \add So\add* Jacob went down to Egypt, \add where\add* he and our fathers died. \v 16 \add Their bones\add* were carried back\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Literally \fqa And they were carried back\f* to Shechem and placed in the tomb that Abraham had bought from the sons of Hamor at Shechem for a price he paid in silver.
\p \v 17 As the time drew near for God \add to fulfill\add* His promise to Abraham, \add our\add* people in Egypt increased greatly in number. \v 18 Then another king, who knew nothing of Joseph, arose over Egypt. \v 19 He exploited our people \add and\add* oppressed our fathers, forcing \add them\add* to abandon their infants so they would die.
\s1 The Birth and Adoption of Moses
\r (\ref Exodus 2:1–10|EXO 2:1-10\ref*; \ref Hebrews 11:23–29|HEB 11:23-29\ref*)
\p \v 20 At that time Moses was born, and he was beautiful in the sight of God.\f + \fr 7:20 \ft Or \fqa he was of great status in God’s eyes \ft or \fqa he was no ordinary child\f* \add For\add* three months \add he\add* was nurtured in \add his\add* father’s house. \v 21 \add When\add* he was set outside, Pharaoh’s daughter took him and brought him up as her own son. \v 22 So Moses was educated in all \add the\add* wisdom of \add the\add* Egyptians and was powerful in speech and action.
\s1 The Rejection and Flight of Moses
\r (\ref Exodus 2:11–22|EXO 2:11-22\ref*)
\p \v 23 When \add Moses was\add* forty years \add old\add*, he decided to visit his brothers, the children of Israel. \v 24 And when he saw one \add of them\add* being mistreated, \add Moses\add* went to his defense and avenged \add him\add* by striking down the Egyptian who was oppressing \add him\add*. \v 25 He assumed his brothers would understand that God was using him to deliver them, but \add they did not\add*.
\p \v 26 The next day he came upon \add two Israelites who\add* were fighting, and he tried to reconcile them, saying, ‘Men, you are brothers. Why are you mistreating each other?’
\p \v 27 But the \add man who\add* was abusing \add his\add* neighbor pushed \add Moses\add* aside \add and\add* said, ‘Who made you ruler and judge over us? \v 28 \add Do\add* you want to kill me as you killed the Egyptian yesterday?’\f + \fr 7:28 \ref Exodus 2:13–14|EXO 2:13-14\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 29 At this remark, Moses fled to \add the\add* land of Midian, where he lived as a foreigner \add and\add* had two sons.
\s1 The Call of Moses
\r (\ref Exodus 3:1–22|EXO 3:1-22\ref*)
\p \v 30 \add After\add* forty years had passed, an angel appeared to \add Moses\add* in \add the\add* flames of a burning bush in the desert near Mount Sinai. \v 31 \add When\add* Moses saw \add it\add*, he marveled at the sight. \add As\add* he approached to look \add more closely\add*, \add the\add* voice of \add the\add* Lord came \add to him\add*: \v 32 ‘I \add am\add* the God of your fathers, the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.’\f + \fr 7:32 \ref Exodus 3:6|EXO 3:6\ref*\f* Moses trembled with fear \add and\add* did not dare to look.
\p \v 33 Then the Lord said to him, ‘Take off \add your\add* sandals, for the place where you are standing is holy ground. \v 34 I have indeed seen the oppression of My people in Egypt. I have heard their groaning and have come down to deliver them. Now come, I will send you back to Egypt.’\f + \fr 7:34 \ref Exodus 3:5–10|EXO 3:5-10\ref*\f*
\p \v 35 This Moses, whom they had rejected with the words, ‘Who made you ruler and judge?’\f + \fr 7:35 \ref Exodus 2:14|EXO 2:14\ref*\f* \add is\add* the one whom God sent to be \add their\add* ruler and redeemer through \add the\add* angel\f + \fr 7:35 \ft Or \fqa Angel\ft ; also in verse 38\f* who appeared to him in the bush. \v 36 \add He\add* led them out \add and\add* performed wonders and signs in \add the\add* land of Egypt, at \add the\add* Red Sea, and \add for\add* forty years in the wilderness.
\p \v 37 This is the \add same\add* Moses who told the Israelites, ‘God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brothers.’\f + \fr 7:37 \ref Deuteronomy 18:15|DEU 18:15\ref*\f* \v 38 \add He\add* was in the assembly in the wilderness with the angel who spoke to him on Mount Sinai, and \add with\add* our fathers. \add And\add* he received living words to pass on to us.\f + \fr 7:38 \ft NE and WH \fqa to you\f*
\s1 The Rebellion of Israel
\r (\ref Exodus 32:1–35|EXO 32:1-35\ref*; \ref Deuteronomy 9:7–29|DEU 9:7-29\ref*; \ref Amos 5:16–27|AMO 5:16-27\ref*)
\p \v 39 \add But\add* our fathers refused to obey \add him\add*. Instead, they rejected \add him\add* and in their hearts turned back to Egypt. \v 40 They said to Aaron, ‘Make us gods who will go before us! As for this Moses who led us out of \add the\add* land of Egypt, we do not know what has happened to him.’\f + \fr 7:40 \ref Exodus 32:1|EXO 32:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 41 At that time they made a calf and offered a sacrifice to the idol, rejoicing in the works of their hands. \v 42 But God turned away \add from them\add* and gave them over to the worship of the host of heaven, as it is written in \add the\add* book of the prophets:
\b 
\q1 ‘Did you bring Me sacrifices and offerings
\q2 forty years in the wilderness, O house of Israel?
\q1 \v 43 You have taken along the tabernacle of Molech
\q2 and the star of your god Rephan,
\q2 the idols you made to worship.
\q1 \add Therefore\add* I will send you into exile
\q2 beyond Babylon.’\f + \fr 7:43 \ref Amos 5:25–27|AMO 5:25-27\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 The Tabernacle of the Testimony
\r (\ref Exodus 40:1–33|EXO 40:1-33\ref*; \ref Hebrews 9:1–10|HEB 9:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 44 Our fathers \add had\add* the tabernacle of the Testimony with \add them\add* in the wilderness. \add It was\add* constructed exactly as \add God\add* had directed Moses, according to the pattern he had seen. \v 45 And our fathers who received it brought \add it in\add* with Joshua \add when\add* they dispossessed the nations God drove out before \add them\add*. \add It remained\add* until the time of David, \v 46 who found favor in the sight of God and asked to provide a dwelling place for the God of Jacob.\f + \fr 7:46 \ft SBL, WH, BYZ, and TR; see also LXX for \ref Psalm 132:5|PSA 132:5\ref*; ECM and NE \fqa a dwelling place for the house of Jacob.\f* \v 47 But \add it was\add* Solomon \add who\add* built \add the\add* house for Him.
\p \v 48 However, the Most High \add does\add* not dwell in \add houses\add* made by human hands. As the prophet says:
\b 
\q1 \v 49 ‘Heaven \add is\add* My throne
\q2 and the earth \add is\add* My footstool.
\q1 What kind of house will you build for Me, says \add the\add* Lord,
\q2 or where \add will\add* My place of repose \add be\add*?
\q1 \v 50 \add Has\add* not My hand made all these things?’\f + \fr 7:50 \ref Isaiah 66:1–2|ISA 66:1-2\ref*\f*
\p \v 51 \add You\add* stiff-necked \add people with\add* uncircumcised hearts and ears! You always resist the Holy Spirit, just as your fathers \add did\add*. \v 52 Which of the prophets \add did\add* your fathers fail to persecute? They even killed those who foretold the coming of the Righteous One. And now you are \add His\add* betrayers and murderers — \v 53 \add you\add* who received the law ordained by angels, yet have not kept \add it\add*.”
\s1 The Stoning of Stephen
\p \v 54 On hearing this, \add the members of the Sanhedrin\add* were enraged,\f + \fr 7:54 \ft Literally \fqa On hearing these things, they were cut in their hearts,\f* and they gnashed \add their\add* teeth at him. \v 55 But \add Stephen\add*, full of \add the\add* Holy Spirit, looked intently into heaven \add and\add* saw \add the\add* glory of God and Jesus standing at the right hand of God. \v 56 “Look,” he said, “I see heaven open and the Son of Man standing at the right \add hand\add* of God.”
\p \v 57 At this they covered their ears, cried out in a loud voice, and rushed together at him. \v 58 They dragged \add him\add* out of the city \add and\add* began to stone \add him\add*. Meanwhile the witnesses laid their garments at the feet of a young man named Saul.
\p \v 59 \add While\add* they were stoning \add him\add*, Stephen appealed, “Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.” \v 60 Falling on \add his\add* knees, he cried out in a loud voice, “Lord, \add do\add* not hold this sin against them.” And when he had said this, he fell asleep.
\c 8
\s1 Saul Persecutes the Church
\p \v 1 And Saul was there, giving approval to \add Stephen’s\add* death.
\p On that day a great persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem, and all except the apostles were scattered throughout Judea and Samaria. \v 2 God-fearing men buried Stephen and mourned deeply over him. \v 3 But Saul began to destroy the church. Going from house to house, he dragged off men and women \add and\add* put \add them\add* in prison.
\s1 Philip in Samaria
\p \v 4 Those who had been scattered preached the word wherever they went. \v 5 Philip went down to \add a\add* city \add in\add* Samaria \add and\add* proclaimed the Christ to them. \v 6 The crowds all paid close attention to Philip’s message and to the signs they saw him perform. \v 7 With loud shrieks, unclean spirits came out of many who were possessed, and many \add of the\add* paralyzed and lame were healed. \v 8 So there was great joy in that city.
\s1 Simon the Sorcerer
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 18:9–14|DEU 18:9-14\ref*)
\p \v 9 Prior to that time, a man named Simon had practiced sorcery in the city and astounded the people of Samaria. He claimed to be someone great, \v 10 \add and\add* all \add the people\add*, from the least to the greatest, heeded \add his words  and\add* said, “This \add man\add* is the divine power called \add the\add* Great \add Power\add*.” \v 11 They paid close attention to him because he had astounded them for a long time with \add his\add* sorcery.
\p \v 12 But when they believed Philip as he preached the gospel of the kingdom of God and the name of Jesus Christ, they were baptized, both men and women. \v 13 Even Simon himself believed and was baptized. He followed Philip \add closely\add* and was astounded \add by the\add* great signs and miracles he observed.
\p \v 14 \add When\add* the apostles in Jerusalem heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent Peter and John to them. \v 15 On their arrival, they prayed for them to receive \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 16 For \add the Holy Spirit\add* had not yet fallen upon any of them; they had simply been baptized into the name of the Lord Jesus. \v 17 Then \add Peter and John\add* laid \add their\add* hands on them, and they received \add the\add* Holy Spirit.
\p \v 18 \add When\add* Simon saw that the Spirit was given through the laying on of the apostles’ hands, he offered them money. \v 19 “Give \add me\add* this power as well,” he said, “so that everyone on whom I lay \add my\add* hands may receive \add the\add* Holy Spirit.”
\p \v 20 But Peter replied, “\add May\add* your silver perish with you, because you thought you could buy the gift of God with money! \v 21 You have no part \add or\add* share in \add our\add* ministry, because your heart is not right before God. \v 22 Repent, therefore, of your wickedness, and pray to the Lord. Perhaps He will forgive you for the intent of your heart. \v 23 For I see that you are poisoned by bitterness and captive to iniquity.”
\p \v 24 Then Simon answered, “Pray to the Lord for me, so that nothing you have said may happen to me.”
\p \v 25 And after \add Peter and John\add* had testified and spoken the word of the Lord, they returned to Jerusalem, preaching the gospel in many of the Samaritan villages.
\s1 Philip and the Ethiopian
\r (\ref Isaiah 53:1–8|ISA 53:1-8\ref*)
\p \v 26 Now an angel of \add the\add* Lord said to Philip, “Get up and go south to the desert road that goes down from Jerusalem to Gaza.” \v 27 \add So\add* he started out, and on his way \add he met\add* an Ethiopian eunuch, a court official in charge of the entire treasury of Candace,\f + \fr 8:27 \ft Greek \fqa Kandakē\f* queen of \add the\add* Ethiopians. \add He\add* had gone to Jerusalem to worship, \v 28 and on his return was sitting in his chariot reading Isaiah the prophet.
\p \v 29 The Spirit said to Philip, “Go over to \add that\add* chariot and stay by \add it\add*.”
\p \v 30 \add So\add* Philip ran up \add and\add* heard \add the man\add* reading Isaiah the prophet. “Do you understand what you are reading?” \add Philip\add* asked.
\p \v 31 “How can I,” he said, “unless someone guides me?” And he invited Philip to come up \add and\add* sit with him.
\p \v 32 \add The eunuch\add* was reading this passage of Scripture:
\b 
\q1 “He was led like a sheep to \add the\add* slaughter,
\q2 and as a lamb before the shearer \add is\add* silent,
\q2 so He did not open His mouth.
\q1 \v 33 In His humiliation He was deprived of justice.
\q2 Who can recount His descendants?
\q2 For His life was removed from the earth.”\f + \fr 8:33 \ref Isaiah 53:7–8|ISA 53:7-8\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 34 “Tell me,” said the eunuch, “who \add is\add* the prophet talking about, himself or someone else?”
\p \v 35 Then Philip began with this very Scripture and told him the good news about Jesus.
\p \v 36 As they traveled along the road \add and\add* came to some water, the eunuch said, “Look, \add here is\add* water! What is \add there\add* to prevent me \add from\add* being baptized?”\f + \fr 8:36 \ft TR includes \fqa \fv 37\fv*And Philip said, “If you believe with all your heart, you may be baptized.” The eunuch replied, “I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.”\f* \v 38 And he gave orders to stop the chariot. Then both Philip and the eunuch went down into the water, and \add Philip\add* baptized him.
\p \v 39 When they came up out of the water, \add the\add* Spirit of \add the\add* Lord carried Philip \add away\add*, and the eunuch saw him no more, \add but\add* went on his way rejoicing. \v 40 But Philip appeared at Azotus and traveled through \add that region\add*, preaching the gospel in all the towns until he came to Caesarea.
\c 9
\s1 The Road to Damascus
\r (\ref Acts 22:1–21|ACT 22:1-21\ref*; \ref Acts 26:1–23|ACT 26:1-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 Meanwhile, Saul \add was\add* still breathing out murderous threats against the disciples of the Lord. He approached the high priest \v 2 and requested letters to the synagogues in Damascus, so that if he found any men \add or\add* women belonging to the Way, he could bring them as prisoners to Jerusalem.
\p \v 3 \add As Saul\add* drew near to Damascus on his journey, suddenly a light from heaven flashed around him. \v 4 He fell to the ground \add and\add* heard a voice say to him, \wj “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute Me?”\wj*
\p \v 5 “Who are You, Lord?” \add Saul\add* asked.
\p \wj “I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting,”\wj* \add He replied\add*.\f + \fr 9:5 \ft TR includes \fqa “It is hard for you to kick against the goads.” \f* \v 6 \wj “Now get up and go into the city, and you will be told what you must do.”\wj*
\p \v 7 The men traveling with \add Saul\add* stood \add there\add* speechless. They heard the voice but did not see \add anyone\add*. \v 8 Saul got up from the ground, but when he opened his eyes he could not see \add a thing\add*.\f + \fr 9:8 \ft Literally \fqa he could see nothing \ft or \fqa he could see no one\f* \add So\add* they led him by the hand into Damascus. \v 9 \add For\add* three days he was without sight, and he did not eat \add or\add* drink \add anything\add*.
\s1 Ananias Baptizes Saul
\p \v 10 In Damascus there was a disciple named Ananias. The Lord spoke to him in a vision, \wj “Ananias!”\wj*
\p “Here I \add am\add*, Lord,” he answered.
\p \v 11 \wj “Get up!”\wj* the Lord \add told\add* him. \wj “Go to \add the\add* house of Judas on Straight Street and ask for \add a man from\add* Tarsus named Saul, for he is praying.\wj* \v 12 \wj In a vision he has seen a man named Ananias come and place \add his\add* hands on him to restore his sight.”\wj*
\p \v 13 But Ananias answered, “Lord, many \add people\add* have told me about this man \add and\add* all \add the\add* harm he has done to Your saints in Jerusalem. \v 14 And now \add he is\add* here with authority from the chief priests to arrest all who call on Your name.”
\p \v 15 \wj “Go!”\wj* said the Lord. \wj “This \add man\add* is My chosen instrument to carry My name before the Gentiles and their kings, and \add before the\add* people of Israel.\wj* \v 16 \wj I will show him how much he must suffer for My name.”\wj*
\p \v 17 \add So\add* Ananias went to the house, and \add when\add* he arrived, he placed \add his\add* hands on \add Saul\add*. “Brother Saul,” he said, “the Lord Jesus, who appeared to you on the road \add as\add* you were coming \add here\add*, has sent me so that you may see again and be filled \add with the\add* Holy Spirit.”
\p \v 18 At that instant, \add something\add* like scales fell from \add Saul’s\add* eyes, and his sight was restored. He got up \add and\add* was baptized, \v 19 and after taking \add some\add* food, he regained his strength. And he spent several days with the disciples in Damascus.
\s1 Saul Preaches at Damascus
\p \v 20 \add Saul\add* promptly began to proclaim Jesus in the synagogues, \add declaring \add*, “He is the Son of God.”
\p \v 21 All who heard \add him\add* were astounded and asked, “Isn’t this the \add man who\add* wreaked havoc in Jerusalem on those \add who\add* call on this name? And hasn’t he come here to take them as prisoners to the chief priests?”
\p \v 22 But Saul was empowered all the more, and he confounded the Jews living in Damascus by proving that \add Jesus\add* is the Christ.
\s1 The Escape from Damascus
\p \v 23 After many days had passed, the Jews conspired to kill him, \v 24 but Saul learned of their plot. Day and night they watched the \add city\add* gates in order to kill him. \v 25 One night, however, his disciples took \add him and\add* lowered \add him\add* in a basket through \add a window \add* in the wall.\f + \fr 9:25 \ft Literally \fqa through the wall\ft ; see \ref 2 Corinthians 11:33|2CO 11:33\ref*.\f*
\s1 Saul in Jerusalem
\p \v 26 \add When Saul\add* arrived in Jerusalem, he tried to join the disciples, \add but\add* they were all afraid of him, not believing that he was a disciple. \v 27 Then Barnabas brought \add him\add* to the apostles and described how \add Saul\add* had seen the Lord, \add who\add* had spoken to him on the road \add to Damascus\add*, and how \add Saul\add* had spoken boldly in \add that city\add* in the name of Jesus.
\p \v 28 \add So Saul\add* stayed with them, moving about freely in Jerusalem \add and\add* speaking boldly in the name of the Lord. \v 29 He talked and debated with the Grecian Jews,\f + \fr 9:29 \ft Or \fqa Hellenists\f* but they tried to kill him. \v 30 \add When\add* the brothers learned of \add this\add*, they took him down to Caesarea and sent him off to Tarsus.
\s1 The Healing of Aeneas
\p \v 31 Then the church throughout Judea, Galilee, and Samaria experienced \add a time of\add* peace. It grew in strength and numbers, living in the fear of the Lord and the encouragement of the Holy Spirit.
\p \v 32 As Peter traveled throughout \add the area\add*, he went to \add visit\add* the saints \add in\add* Lydda. \v 33 There he found a man named Aeneas who had been paralyzed \add and\add* bedridden for eight years. \v 34 “Aeneas,” Peter said to him, “Jesus Christ heals you! Get up and put away your mat.” Immediately \add Aeneas\add* got up, \v 35 and all who lived in Lydda and Sharon saw him \add and\add* turned to the Lord.
\s1 The Raising of Tabitha
\r (\ref John 11:38–44|JHN 11:38-44\ref*)
\p \v 36 In Joppa there was a disciple named Tabitha (which is translated as Dorcas),\f + \fr 9:36 \fqa Tabitha \ft in Aramaic and \fqa Dorcas \ft in Greek both mean \fqa gazelle\ft .\f* who was always occupied with works of kindness and charity. \v 37 At that time, however, she became sick \add and\add* died, and her body was washed \add and\add* placed in an upper room. \v 38 \add Since\add* Lydda was near Joppa, the disciples, hearing that Peter was \add there\add*, sent two men to urge him, “Come to us without delay.”
\p \v 39 \add So\add* Peter got up \add and\add* went with them. On his arrival, they took \add him\add* to the upper room. All the widows stood around him, weeping and showing \add him the\add* tunics and \add other\add* clothing that Dorcas had made while she was still with them.
\p \v 40 Then Peter sent them all out of the room. He knelt down \add and\add* prayed, and turning toward \add her\add* body, he said, “Tabitha, get up!” She opened her eyes, and seeing Peter, she sat up. \v 41 \add Peter\add* took her by the hand \add and\add* helped her up. Then he called the saints and widows \add and\add* presented her \add to them\add* alive.
\p \v 42 \add This\add* became known all over Joppa, and many \add people\add* believed in the Lord. \v 43 And \add Peter\add* stayed for several days in Joppa with a tanner \add named\add* Simon.
\c 10
\s1 Cornelius Sends for Peter
\p \v 1 At Caesarea \add there was\add* a man named Cornelius, a centurion in what was called \add the\add* Italian Regiment. \v 2 \add He and\add* all his household \add were\add* devout and God-fearing. He gave generously to the people and prayed to God regularly. \v 3 \add One\add* day at about the ninth hour,\f + \fr 10:3 \ft That is, about three in the afternoon; also in verse 30\f* he had a clear vision of an angel of God who came to him and said, “Cornelius!”
\p \v 4 \add Cornelius\add* stared at him in fear and asked, “What is it, Lord?”
\p \add The angel\add* answered, “Your prayers and gifts to the poor have ascended as a memorial \add offering \add* before God. \v 5 Now send men to Joppa to call for a man \add named\add* Simon who is called Peter. \v 6 He is staying with Simon \add the\add* tanner, whose house is by \add the\add* sea.\f + \fr 10:6 \ft TR includes \fqa He will tell you what you need to do.\f*”
\p \v 7 When the angel who spoke to him had gone, \add Cornelius\add* called two of \add his\add* servants and a devout soldier from among his attendants. \v 8 He explained what had happened \add and\add* sent them to Joppa.
\s1 Peter’s Vision
\r (\ref Leviticus 11:1–47|LEV 11:1-47\ref*; \ref Deuteronomy 14:1–21|DEU 14:1-21\ref*)
\p \v 9 The next day at about the sixth hour,\f + \fr 10:9 \ft That is, about noon\f* as \add the men\add* were approaching the city on their journey, Peter went up on the roof to pray. \v 10 He became hungry and wanted \add something\add* to eat, but \add while\add* the meal was being prepared, he fell into a trance.
\p \v 11 He saw heaven open and something like a large sheet being let down to earth by its four corners. \v 12 It contained all kinds of four-footed animals and reptiles of the earth, as well as birds of the air. \v 13 Then a voice said to him: \wj “Get up, Peter, kill and eat!”\wj*
\p \v 14 “No, Lord!” Peter answered. “I have never eaten anything impure\f + \fr 10:14 \ft Literally \fqa common\ft ; similarly in verses 15 and 28\f* or unclean.”
\p \v 15 \add The\add* voice \add spoke\add* to him a second time: \wj “Do not call anything impure that God has made clean.”\wj*
\p \v 16 This happened three times, and all at once the \add sheet\add* was taken back up into heaven.
\s1 Peter Called to Caesarea
\p \v 17 While Peter was puzzling over the meaning of the vision, the men sent by Cornelius found Simon’s house \add and\add* approached the gate. \v 18 They called out to ask if Simon called Peter was staying \add there\add*.
\p \v 19 As Peter continued to reflect on the vision, the Spirit said to him, “Behold, three men are looking for you.\f + \fr 10:19 \ft ECM and TR; NE and WH \fqa two men are looking for you\ft ; SBL and BYZ \fqa men are looking for you\f* \v 20 So get up! Go downstairs and accompany them without hesitation, because I have sent them.”
\p \v 21 \add So\add* Peter went down to the men\f + \fr 10:21 \ft TR \fqa the men sent to him by Cornelius\f* \add and\add* said, “Here am I, the one you are looking for. Why have you come?”
\p \v 22 “Cornelius \add the\add* centurion \add has sent us \add*,” they said. “\add He is\add* a righteous and God-fearing man \add with\add* a good reputation among the whole Jewish nation. A holy angel instructed \add him\add* to request your presence in his home \add so he could\add* hear a message from you.”
\p \v 23 So \add Peter\add* invited them in as his guests. And the next day he got ready \add and\add* went with them, accompanied by some of the brothers from Joppa.
\s1 Peter Visits Cornelius
\p \v 24 The following day he arrived in Caesarea, \add where\add* Cornelius was expecting them \add and\add* had called together his relatives and close friends. \v 25 As Peter was about to enter, Cornelius met him \add and\add* fell at \add his\add* feet to worship \add him\add*. \v 26 But Peter helped him up. “Stand up,” he said, “I am only a man myself.”
\p \v 27 \add As Peter\add* talked with him, he went inside and found many \add people\add* gathered together. \v 28 He said to them, “You know how unlawful it is for a Jew to associate with a foreigner or visit \add him\add*. But God has shown me \add that\add* I should not call \add any\add* man impure or unclean. \v 29 So when I was invited, I came without objection. I ask, then, why have you sent for me?”
\p \v 30 Cornelius answered: “Four days ago I was in my house praying at this, the ninth hour.\f + \fr 10:30 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa Four days ago I was fasting until this hour, and at the ninth hour I was praying in my house.\f* Suddenly a man in radiant clothing stood before me \v 31 and said, ‘Cornelius, your prayer has been heard, and your gifts to the poor have been remembered before God. \v 32 Therefore send to Joppa for Simon, who is called Peter. He is a guest in \add the\add* home of Simon \add the\add* tanner, by \add the\add* sea.’
\p \v 33 So I sent for you immediately, and you were kind enough to come. Now then, we \add are\add* all here in the presence of God to listen to everything the Lord has instructed you \add to tell us\add*.”
\s1 Good News for the Gentiles
\p \v 34 Then Peter began to speak: “I now truly understand that God does not show favoritism, \v 35 but welcomes those from every nation who fear Him and do what is right. \v 36 He has sent \add this\add* message to the people of Israel, proclaiming the gospel of peace through Jesus Christ, \add who\add* is Lord of all.
\p \v 37 You yourselves know what has happened throughout Judea, beginning in Galilee with the baptism that John proclaimed: \v 38 how God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with \add the\add* Holy Spirit and with power, \add and\add* how \add Jesus\add* went around doing good and healing all who were oppressed by the devil, because God was with Him.
\p \v 39 We \add are\add* witnesses of all that He did, both in the land of the Jews and in Jerusalem. And although they put \add Him\add* to death by hanging \add Him\add* on a tree, \v 40 God raised \add Him\add* up on the third day and caused Him to be seen — \v 41 not by all the people, but by the witnesses God had chosen beforehand, by us who ate and drank with Him after He rose from \add the\add* dead. \v 42 And He commanded us to preach to the people and to testify that He is the \add One\add* appointed by God to judge \add the\add* living and \add the\add* dead. \v 43 All the prophets testify about Him \add that\add* everyone who believes in Him receives forgiveness of sins through His name.”
\s1 The Gentiles Receive the Holy Spirit
\r (\ref Acts 19:1–7|ACT 19:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 44 \add While\add* Peter was still speaking these words, the Holy Spirit fell upon all who heard \add his\add* message. \v 45 All the circumcised believers \add who\add* had accompanied Peter were astounded that the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out even on the Gentiles. \v 46 For they heard them speaking in tongues and exalting God.
\p Then Peter said, \v 47 “Can anyone withhold the water to baptize these \add people\add*? \add They\add* have received the Holy Spirit just as we \add have\add*!” \v 48 So he ordered that they be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ. Then they asked him to stay \add for a few\add* days.
\c 11
\s1 Peter’s Report at Jerusalem
\p \v 1 The apostles and brothers throughout Judea soon heard that the Gentiles also had received the word of God. \v 2 \add So\add* when Peter went up to Jerusalem, \add the\add* circumcised \add believers\add*\f + \fr 11:2 \ft Literally \fqa those of the circumcision\f* took issue with him \v 3 \add and\add* said, “You visited uncircumcised men and ate with them.”
\p \v 4 But Peter began \add and\add* explained to them the whole sequence of events: \v 5 “I was in \add the\add* city of Joppa praying, and in a trance I saw a vision \add of\add* something like a large sheet being let down from heaven by \add its\add* four corners, and it came right down to me. \v 6 I looked at it \add closely and\add* saw four-footed animals of the earth, wild beasts, reptiles, and birds of the air. \v 7 Then I heard a voice saying to me, \wj ‘Get up, Peter, kill and eat.’\wj*
\p \v 8 ‘No, Lord,’ I said, ‘for nothing impure\f + \fr 11:8 \ft Literally \fqa common\ft ; similarly in verse 9\f* or unclean has \add ever\add* entered my mouth.’
\p \v 9 But the voice spoke from heaven a second \add time\add*, \wj ‘Do not call anything impure that God has made clean.’\wj*
\p \v 10 This happened three times, and everything was drawn back up into heaven.
\p \v 11 Just then three men sent to me from Caesarea stopped at the house \add where\add* I was \add staying\add*. \v 12 The Spirit told me to accompany them without hesitation. These six brothers also went with me, and we entered the man’s home. \v 13 He told us how he had seen an angel standing in his house and saying, ‘Send to Joppa \add for\add* Simon who is called Peter. \v 14 \add He\add* will convey to you a message by which you and all your household will be saved.’
\p \v 15 \add As\add* I began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell upon them, just as \add He had fallen\add* upon us at \add the\add* beginning. \v 16 Then I remembered the word of the Lord, how He said, \wj ‘John baptized with water, but you will be baptized with \add the\add* Holy Spirit.’\f + \fr 11:16 \ft Or \fqa ‘John baptized in water, but in a few days you will be baptized in the Holy Spirit.’\ref Acts 1:5|ACT 1:5\ref*\f*\wj* \v 17 So if God gave them the same gift \add He gave\add* us who believed in the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I to hinder \add the work of\add* God?”
\p \v 18 \add When\add* they heard \add this\add*, they had no further \add objections\add*, and they glorified God, saying, “So then, God has granted even the Gentiles repentance unto life.”
\s1 The Church at Antioch
\p \v 19 Meanwhile those scattered by the persecution that began with Stephen traveled as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus, and Antioch, speaking the message only to Jews. \v 20 But some of them, men from Cyprus and Cyrene, went to Antioch and began speaking to the Greeks\f + \fr 11:20 \ft Or \fqa the Hellenists\f* as well, proclaiming the good news about the Lord Jesus. \v 21 \add The\add* hand of \add the\add* Lord was with them, and \add a\add* great number \add of people\add* believed \add and\add* turned to the Lord.
\p \v 22 \add When\add* news of this \add reached\add* the ears of the church in Jerusalem, they sent Barnabas to Antioch. \v 23 \add When\add* he arrived and saw the grace of God, he rejoiced and encouraged them all to abide in the Lord with all their hearts. \v 24 \add Barnabas\add* was a good man, full of \add the\add* Holy Spirit and faith, and a great \add number of people\add* were brought to the Lord.
\p \v 25 Then \add Barnabas\add* went to Tarsus to look for Saul, \v 26 and \add when\add* he found \add him\add*, he brought \add him\add* back to Antioch. \add So for\add* a full year they met together with the church and taught large \add numbers of people\add*. The disciples were first called Christians at Antioch.
\p \v 27 In those days \add some\add* prophets came down from Jerusalem to Antioch. \v 28 One of them named Agabus stood up \add and\add* predicted through the Spirit \add that\add* a great famine would sweep across the whole world.\f + \fr 11:28 \ft Or \fqa the entire Roman world\f* (\add This\add* happened under Claudius.) \v 29 \add So\add* the disciples, each according to his ability, decided to send relief to the brothers living in Judea. \v 30 This they did, sending \add their gifts\add* to the elders with Barnabas and Saul.
\c 12
\s1 James Killed, Peter Imprisoned
\p \v 1 About that time, King Herod\f + \fr 12:1 \ft That is, King Herod Agrippa\f* reached out to harm\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Literally \fqa put forth the hands to mistreat\f* some who belonged to the church. \v 2 \add He had\add* James, the brother of John, put to death with the sword.
\p \v 3 And seeing that \add this\add* pleased the Jews, \add Herod\add* proceeded to seize Peter during the Feast of Unleavened \add Bread\add*.\f + \fr 12:3 \ft Literally \fqa seize Peter—now these were the days of the Unleavened\ft ; see \ref Exodus 12:14–20|EXO 12:14-20\ref*.\f* \v 4 He arrested \add him\add* and put \add him\add* in prison, handing \add him\add* over to be guarded by four squads of four soldiers \add each\add*. \add Herod\add* intended to bring him out to the people after the Passover.
\s1 The Rescue of Peter
\p \v 5 So Peter was kept in prison, but the church was fervently praying to God for him.
\p \v 6 On \add the\add* night \add before\add* Herod was to bring him \add to trial\add*, Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains, with sentries standing guard at the entrance to the prison. \v 7 Suddenly an angel of \add the\add* Lord appeared and a light shone in the cell. He tapped Peter on the side \add and\add* woke him up, saying, “Get up quickly.” And the chains fell off \add his\add* wrists. \v 8 “Get dressed and put on your sandals,” said the angel. Peter did so, and \add the angel\add* told him, “Wrap your cloak \add around you\add* and follow me.”
\p \v 9 \add So Peter\add* followed him out, \add but\add* he was unaware that what the angel was doing was real. He thought he was only seeing a vision. \v 10 They passed \add the\add* first and second guards \add and\add* came to the iron gate leading to the city, which opened for them by itself. \add When\add* they had gone outside \add and\add* walked the length of one block, the angel suddenly left him.
\p \v 11 Then Peter came to himself \add and\add* said, “Now I know for sure that the Lord has sent His angel and rescued me from Herod’s grasp and \add from\add* everything the Jewish people were anticipating.”
\p \v 12 And \add when\add* he had realized \add this\add*, he went to the house of Mary the mother of John, \add also\add* called Mark, where many \add people\add* had gathered together and were praying. \v 13 He knocked \add at\add* the outer gate, \add and\add* a servant girl named Rhoda came to answer \add it\add*. \v 14 When she recognized Peter’s voice, she was so overjoyed \add that\add* she forgot to open the gate, but ran inside \add and\add* announced, “Peter is standing at the gate!”
\p \v 15 “You are out of your mind,” they told her. But \add when\add* she kept insisting \add it\add* was so, they said, “It must be his angel.”
\p \v 16 But Peter kept on knocking, and \add when\add* they opened \add the door and\add* saw him, they were astounded. \v 17 \add Peter\add* motioned with \add his\add* hand for silence, \add and\add* he described how the Lord had brought him out of the prison. “Send word to James and to the brothers,” he said, and he left for another place.
\p \v 18 At daybreak there was no small commotion among the soldiers as to what had become \add of\add* Peter. \v 19 \add After\add* Herod had searched for him \add unsuccessfully\add*, he examined the guards \add and\add* ordered that they be executed. Then he went down from Judea to Caesarea \add and\add* spent some time \add there\add*.
\s1 The Death of Herod
\p \v 20 Now \add Herod\add* was in a furious dispute\f + \fr 12:20 \ft Or \fqa had become furious \f* \add with the\add* people of Tyre and Sidon, and they convened before him. Having secured the support of Blastus, the king’s chamberlain, they asked for peace, because their region depended on the king’s \add country\add* for food. \v 21 On the appointed day, Herod donned \add his\add* royal \add robes\add*, sat on \add his\add* throne, \add and\add* addressed \add the people\add*. \v 22 And \add they\add* began to shout, “\add This is the\add* voice of a god, not a man!”
\p \v 23 Immediately, because \add Herod\add* did not give glory to God, an angel of \add the\add* Lord struck him \add down\add*, and he was eaten by worms \add and\add* died.
\p \v 24 But the word of God continued to spread and multiply.
\p \v 25 \add When\add* Barnabas and Saul had fulfilled \add their\add* mission to Jerusalem, they returned,\f + \fr 12:25 \ft Or \fqa had fulfilled their mission, they returned to Jerusalem\ft ; NE and TR \fqa had fulfilled their mission, they returned from Jerusalem\f* bringing with \add them\add* John, \add also\add* called Mark.
\c 13
\s1 Paul’s First Missionary Journey Begins
\r (\ref Acts 15:36–41|ACT 15:36-41\ref*; \ref Acts 18:23–28|ACT 18:23-28\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now in the church at Antioch there were prophets and teachers: Barnabas, Simeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen (who had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch), and Saul. \v 2 \add While\add* they were worshiping the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, “Set apart for Me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them.” \v 3 And after they had fasted and prayed, they laid \add their\add* hands on them \add and\add* sent \add them\add* off.
\s1 On Cyprus
\p \v 4 So \add Barnabas and Saul\add*, sent forth by the Holy Spirit, went down to Seleucia and sailed from there to Cyprus. \v 5 \add When\add* they arrived at Salamis, they proclaimed the word of God in the Jewish synagogues. And John was with them as their helper.
\p \v 6 They traveled through the whole island as far as Paphos, \add where\add* they found a Jewish sorcerer \add and\add* false prophet named Bar-Jesus, \v 7 an attendant of the proconsul, Sergius Paulus. \add The proconsul\add*, a man of intelligence, summoned Barnabas and Saul because he wanted to hear the word of God. \v 8 But Elymas the sorcerer (for that is what his name means) opposed them \add and\add* tried to turn the proconsul from the faith.
\p \v 9 Then Saul, who \add was\add* also \add called\add* Paul, filled with \add the\add* Holy Spirit, looked directly at Elymas \v 10 and said, “O child of \add the\add* devil \add and\add* enemy of all righteousness, \add you are\add* full of all kinds of deceit and trickery! Will you never stop perverting the straight ways of the Lord? \v 11 Now look, \add the\add* hand of the Lord \add is\add* against you, and for a time you will be blind \add and\add* unable to see the light of the sun.” Immediately mist and darkness came over him, and he groped about, seeking someone to lead \add him\add* by the hand.
\p \v 12 \add When\add* the proconsul saw what had happened, he believed, for he was astonished at the teaching about the Lord.
\s1 In Pisidian Antioch
\p \v 13 After setting sail from Paphos, Paul \add and\add* his companions came to Perga in Pamphylia, \add where\add* John left them to return to Jerusalem. \v 14 And from Perga, they traveled inland to Pisidian Antioch, where they entered the synagogue on the Sabbath \add and\add* sat down. \v 15 After the reading from the Law and the Prophets, the synagogue leaders sent word to them: “Brothers, if you have a word of encouragement for the people, please speak.”
\p \v 16 Paul stood up, motioned with \add his\add* hand, \add and\add* began to speak: “Men \add of\add* Israel and \add you Gentiles\add* who fear God, listen \add to me\add*! \v 17 The God of the people of Israel chose our fathers. He made them into a great people during \add their\add* stay in Egypt, and with an uplifted arm He led them out of \add that land\add*. \v 18 He endured their conduct for about forty years in the wilderness. \v 19 And having vanquished seven nations in Canaan, \add He gave\add* their land \add to His people\add* as an inheritance. \v 20 \add All this took about\add* 450 years.
\p After this, \add God\add* gave \add them\add* judges until the time of Samuel the prophet. \v 21 Then \add the people\add* asked for a king, and God gave them Saul son of Kish, from \add the\add* tribe of Benjamin, \add who ruled\add* forty years. \v 22 \add After\add* removing \add Saul\add*, He raised up David as their king and testified \add about him\add*: ‘I have found David \add son\add* of Jesse a man after My \add own\add* heart; \add he\add* will carry out My will in its entirety.’\f + \fr 13:22 \ft See \ref 1 Samuel 13:14|1SA 13:14\ref*.\f*
\p \v 23 From the descendants of this \add man\add*, God has brought to Israel \add the\add* Savior Jesus, as He promised. \v 24 Before the arrival of \add Jesus\add*, John preached a baptism of repentance to all the people of Israel. \v 25 As John was completing \add his\add* course, he said, ‘Who do you suppose I am? I am not \add that One\add*. But there is One coming after me whose sandals I am not worthy to untie.’\f + \fr 13:25 \ref Luke 3:16|LUK 3:16\ref*; see also Matthew 3:11, Mark 1:7, and John 1:27.\f*
\p \v 26 Brothers, children of Abraham, and you  \add Gentiles \add* who fear God, \add it is\add* to us \add that\add* this message of salvation has been sent. \v 27 The \add people\add* of Jerusalem and their rulers did not recognize \add Jesus\add*, yet in condemning \add Him\add* they fulfilled the words of the prophets that are read every Sabbath. \v 28 And though they found no ground for a death sentence, they asked Pilate to have Him executed.
\p \v 29 When they had carried out all that was written about Him, they took \add Him\add* down from the tree \add and\add* laid \add Him\add* in a tomb. \v 30 But God raised Him from \add the\add* dead, \v 31 \add and\add* for many days \add He\add* was seen by those who had accompanied Him from Galilee to Jerusalem. \add They\add* are now His witnesses to \add our\add* people.
\p \v 32 And now we proclaim to you the good news: \add What God\add* promised \add our\add* fathers \v 33 \add He\add* has fulfilled for us, their children, by raising up Jesus. As it is written in the second Psalm:
\b 
\q1 ‘You are My Son;
\q2 today I have become Your Father.’\f + \fr 13:33 \ref Psalm 2:7|PSA 2:7\ref*; literally \fqa today I have begotten You\f*
\p \v 34 In fact, \add God\add* raised Him from \add the\add* dead, never to see decay. As He has said:
\b 
\q1 ‘I will give you the holy \add and\add* sure \add blessings promised to\add* David.’\f + \fr 13:34 \ft Literally \fqa I will give you the holy, the trustworthy of David\ft ; \ref Isaiah 55:3|ISA 55:3\ref*\f*
\p \v 35 So also, He says in another \add Psalm\add*:
\b 
\q1 ‘You will not let Your Holy One see decay.’\f + \fr 13:35 \ref Psalm 16:10|PSA 16:10\ref*\f*
\p \v 36 For \add when\add* David had served God’s purpose in his own generation, he fell asleep. \add His body\add* was buried with his fathers and saw decay. \v 37 But the \add One\add* whom God raised from the dead did not see decay.
\p \v 38 Therefore let it be known to you, brothers, that through \add Jesus the\add* forgiveness of sins is proclaimed to you. \v 39 Through Him everyone who believes is justified from everything from which you could not be justified by \add the\add* law of Moses. \v 40 Watch out, then, that what was spoken by the prophets does not happen \add to you\add*:
\b 
\q1 \v 41 ‘Look, \add you\add* scoffers,
\q2 wonder and perish!
\q1 For I am doing a work in your days
\q2 \add that\add* you would never believe,
\q2 even if someone told you.’\f + \fr 13:41 \ref Habakkuk 1:5|HAB 1:5\ref* (see also LXX)\f*”
\s1 A Light for the Gentiles
\r (\ref Isaiah 49:1–6|ISA 49:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 42 \add As Paul and Barnabas\add* were leaving \add the synagogue\add*, \add the people\add* urged them to continue this message on the next Sabbath. \v 43 \add After\add* the synagogue was dismissed, many of the Jews and devout converts to Judaism followed Paul and Barnabas, who spoke to them \add and\add* urged them to continue in the grace of God.
\p \v 44 On the following Sabbath, nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of \add the\add* Lord. \v 45 But \add when\add* the Jews saw the crowds, they were filled with jealousy, and \add they\add* blasphemously contradicted what Paul was saying.
\p \v 46 Then Paul and Barnabas answered \add them\add* boldly: “It was necessary to speak the word of God to you first. But since you reject it and do not consider yourselves worthy of eternal life, we now turn to the Gentiles. \v 47 For this is what the Lord has commanded us:
\b 
\q1 ‘I have made you a light for \add the\add* Gentiles,
\q2 to bring salvation to \add the\add* ends of the earth.’\f + \fr 13:47 \ref Isaiah 49:6|ISA 49:6\ref*\f*”
\p \v 48 \add When\add* the Gentiles heard \add this\add*, they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord, and all who were appointed for eternal life believed. \v 49 And the word of the Lord spread throughout \add that\add* region.
\p \v 50 The Jews, however, incited the religious women of prominence and the leading men of the city. They stirred up persecution against Paul and Barnabas and drove them out of their district. \v 51 \add So\add* they shook the dust \add off their\add* feet \add in protest\add* against them \add and\add* went to Iconium. \v 52 And the disciples were filled with joy and \add with the\add* Holy Spirit.
\c 14
\s1 Paul and Barnabas at Iconium
\p \v 1 At Iconium, \add Paul and Barnabas\add* went as usual into the Jewish synagogue, \add where\add* they spoke so \add well \add* that a great number of Jews and Greeks believed. \v 2 But the unbelieving Jews stirred up the Gentiles and poisoned \add their\add* minds against the brothers. \v 3 So \add Paul and Barnabas\add* spent considerable time \add there\add*, speaking boldly for the Lord, who affirmed the message of His grace by enabling them to perform signs and wonders.
\p \v 4 The people of the city were divided. \add Some\add* sided with the Jews, and \add others\add* with the apostles. \v 5 But when the Gentiles and Jews, together with their rulers, set out to mistreat and stone them, \v 6 they found out about it and fled to the Lycaonian cities of Lystra and Derbe and to the surrounding region, \v 7 where they continued to preach the gospel.
\s1 The Visit to Lystra and Derbe
\p \v 8 In Lystra there sat a man crippled in \add his\add* feet, who was lame from birth \add and had\add* never walked. \v 9 This \add man\add* was listening to the words of Paul, who looked intently at him and saw that he had faith to be healed. \v 10 In a loud voice \add Paul\add* called out, “Stand up on your feet!” And \add the man\add* jumped up and began to walk.
\p \v 11 \add When\add* the crowds saw what Paul had done, they lifted up their voices in the Lycaonian language: “The gods have come down to us in human form!” \v 12 Barnabas they called Zeus, and Paul \add they called\add* Hermes, because he was the chief speaker. \v 13 The priest of Zeus, \add whose temple\add* was just outside the city, brought bulls and wreaths to the \add city\add* gates, hoping to offer a sacrifice along with the crowds.
\p \v 14 But \add when\add* the apostles Barnabas and Paul found out about this, they tore their clothes \add and\add* rushed into the crowd, shouting, \v 15 “Men, why are you doing this? We too are \add only\add* men, human like you. We are bringing you good news that you should turn from these worthless things to \add the\add* living God, who made heaven and earth and sea and everything in them. \v 16 In past generations, \add He\add* let all nations go their \add own\add* way. \v 17 Yet He has not left Himself without testimony to His goodness: He gives you rain from heaven and fruitful seasons, filling your hearts with food and gladness.”
\p \v 18 Even with these words, \add Paul and Barnabas\add* could hardly stop the crowds from sacrificing to them.
\p \v 19 Then \add some\add* Jews arrived from Antioch and Iconium and won over the crowds. They stoned Paul \add and\add* dragged \add him\add* outside the city, presuming he was dead. \v 20 But \add after\add* the disciples had gathered around him, he got up and went back into the city. And the next day he left with Barnabas for Derbe.
\s1 Strengthening the Disciples
\p \v 21 They preached the gospel to that city and made many disciples. \add Then\add* they returned to Lystra, Iconium, and Antioch, \v 22 strengthening the souls of the disciples \add and\add* encouraging \add them\add* to continue in the faith. “We must endure many hardships to enter the kingdom of God,” \add they said\add*.
\p \v 23 \add Paul and Barnabas\add* appointed elders for them in each church, praying \add and\add* fasting as they entrusted them to the Lord, in whom they had believed.
\p \v 24 \add After\add* passing through Pisidia, they came to Pamphylia. \v 25 And \add when\add* they had spoken the word in Perga, they went down to Attalia.
\p \v 26 From \add Attalia\add* they sailed to Antioch, where they had been commended to the grace of God for the work they had just completed. \v 27 \add When\add* they arrived, they gathered the church \add together and\add* reported all that God had done through them, and how He had opened \add the\add* door of faith to the Gentiles. \v 28 And they spent a long time \add there\add* with the disciples.
\c 15
\s1 The Dispute over Circumcision
\p \v 1 Then some men came down from Judea \add and\add* were teaching the brothers, “Unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.” \v 2 And after engaging \add these men\add* in sharp debate, Paul and Barnabas were appointed, along with some other \add believers \add*, to go up to Jerusalem to \add see\add* the apostles and elders about this question.
\p \v 3 Sent on their way by the church, they passed through Phoenicia and Samaria, recounting the conversion of the Gentiles and bringing great joy to all the brothers. \v 4 \add On\add* their arrival in Jerusalem, they were welcomed by the church and apostles and elders, \add to whom\add* they reported all that God had done through them.
\s1 The Council at Jerusalem
\r (\ref Amos 9:11–15|AMO 9:11-15\ref*; \ref Galatians 2:1–10|GAL 2:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 5 But some believers from the party of the Pharisees stood up \add and\add* declared, “\add The Gentiles\add* must be circumcised and required to obey the law of Moses.” \v 6 \add So\add* the apostles and elders met to look into this matter.
\p \v 7 After much discussion, Peter got up \add and\add* said to them, “Brothers, you know that in \add the\add* early days God made a choice among you \add that\add* the Gentiles would hear from my lips the message of the gospel and believe. \v 8 And God, who knows the heart, showed His approval by giving the Holy Spirit to them, just as \add He did\add* to us. \v 9 He made no distinction between us and them, \add for\add* He cleansed their hearts by faith.
\p \v 10 Now then, why do you test God by placing on the necks of the disciples a yoke that neither we nor our fathers have been able to bear? \v 11 On the contrary, we believe \add it is\add* through the grace of \add the\add* Lord Jesus \add that\add* we are saved, just as they \add are\add*.”
\p \v 12 The whole assembly fell silent \add as they\add* listened to Barnabas and Paul describing \add the\add* signs and wonders God had done among the Gentiles through them. \v 13 When they had finished speaking, James declared, “Brothers, listen to me! \v 14 Simon\f + \fr 15:14 \ft Greek \fqa Simeon\ft , a variant of Simon\f* has told us how God first visited \add the Gentiles\add* to take from \add them\add* a people \add to be\add* His \add own\add*. \v 15 The words of the prophets agree with this, as it is written:
\b 
\q1 \v 16 ‘After \add this\add* I will return and rebuild
\q2 the fallen tent of David.
\q1 Its ruins I will rebuild,
\q2 and I will restore it,
\q1 \v 17 so that the remnant of men may seek the Lord,
\q2 and all the Gentiles who are called by My name,
\q1 says \add the\add* Lord who does these things
\q2 \v 18 \add that\add* have been known for ages.’\f + \fr 15:18 \ref Amos 9:11–12|AMO 9:11-12\ref* (see also LXX); BYZ and TR \fqa says the Lord, who does all these things. \fv 18\fv*Known unto God are all His works from the ages.\f*
\p \v 19 \add It is\add* my judgment, therefore, that we should not cause trouble for the Gentiles \add who\add* are turning to God. \v 20 Instead, we should write  \add and tell\add* them to abstain from \add food \add* polluted by idols, \add from\add* sexual immorality, \add from\add* the meat of strangled animals, and from blood. \v 21 For Moses has been proclaimed in every city from ancient times \add and\add* is read in the synagogues on every Sabbath.”
\s1 The Letter to the Gentile Believers
\p \v 22 Then the apostles and elders, with the whole church, decided to select men from among them to send to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas. \add They chose\add* Judas called Barsabbas and Silas, \add two\add* leaders among the brothers, \v 23 \add and sent them with\add* this letter:
\lf The apostles and the elders, \add your\add* brothers,
\lf To the brothers among \add the\add* Gentiles in Antioch, Syria, and Cilicia:
\lf Greetings.
\lf \v 24 It has come to our attention that some went out from us without our authorization \add and\add* unsettled you, troubling your minds by what \add they\add* said.\f + \fr 15:24 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa by saying that you must be circumcised and keep the law.\f* \v 25 \add So\add* we all agreed to choose men to send to you along with our beloved Barnabas and Paul, \v 26 men who have risked their lives for the name of our Lord Jesus Christ. \v 27 Therefore we are sending Judas and Silas to tell you \add in person\add* the same things \add we are writing\add*.
\lf \v 28 It seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us not to burden you \add with anything\add* beyond these essential requirements: \v 29 \add You must\add* abstain from food sacrificed to idols, from blood, from the meat of strangled animals, and from sexual immorality. You will do well to avoid these things.
\lf Farewell.
\s1 The Believers at Antioch Rejoice
\p \v 30 So \add the men\add* were sent off \add and\add* went down to Antioch, \add where\add* they assembled the congregation \add and\add* delivered the letter. \v 31 \add When the people\add* read \add it\add*, they rejoiced at \add its\add* encouraging message.
\p \v 32 Judas and Silas, \add who\add* themselves were prophets, said much to encourage and strengthen the brothers. \v 33 After spending some time \add there\add*, they were sent off by the brothers in peace to \add return to\add* those who had sent them.\f + \fr 15:33 \ft TR includes \fqa \fv 34\fv*Silas, however, decided to remain there.\f* \v 35 But Paul and Barnabas remained at Antioch, along with many others, teaching and preaching the word of the Lord.
\s1 Paul’s Second Missionary Journey Begins
\r (\ref Acts 13:1–3|ACT 13:1-3\ref*; \ref Acts 18:23–28|ACT 18:23-28\ref*)
\p \v 36 Some time later Paul said to Barnabas, “Let us go back \add and\add* visit the brothers in every town where we proclaimed the word of the Lord, \add to see \add* how they are doing.” \v 37 Barnabas wanted to take John, \add also\add* called Mark. \v 38 But Paul thought it best not to take him, \add because\add* he had deserted them in Pamphylia and had not accompanied them in the work.
\p \v 39 Their disagreement was so sharp that they parted company. Barnabas took Mark \add and\add* sailed for Cyprus, \v 40 but Paul chose Silas \add and\add* left, commended by the brothers to the grace of the Lord. \v 41 And he traveled through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches.
\c 16
\s1 Timothy Joins Paul and Silas
\p \v 1 \add Paul\add* came to Derbe and then to Lystra, \add where he found\add* a disciple named Timothy, \add the\add* son of a believing Jewish woman and a Greek father. \v 2 The brothers in Lystra and Iconium spoke well of \add him\add*. \v 3 Paul wanted \add Timothy\add* to accompany him, \add so\add* he took \add him and\add* circumcised him on account of the Jews in that area, for they all knew that his father was a Greek.
\p \v 4 As they went from town to town, they delivered the decisions handed down by the apostles and elders in Jerusalem for \add the people\add* to obey. \v 5 So the churches were strengthened in the faith and grew daily in numbers.
\s1 Paul’s Vision of the Macedonian
\p \v 6 \add After\add* the Holy Spirit had prevented them from speaking the word in \add the province of\add* Asia,\f + \fr 16:6 \ft Literally \fqa in Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* they traveled through the region of Phrygia and Galatia. \v 7 And when they came to the border of Mysia, they tried to enter Bithynia, \add but\add* the Spirit of Jesus would not permit them. \v 8 \add So\add* they passed by Mysia \add and\add* went down to Troas.
\p \v 9 During the night, Paul had a vision of a man of Macedonia standing and pleading with him, “Come over to Macedonia \add and\add* help us.” \v 10 As soon as \add Paul\add* had seen the vision, we got ready to leave for Macedonia, concluding that God had called us to preach the gospel to them.
\s1 Lydia’s Conversion in Philippi
\r (\ref Revelation 2:18–29|REV 2:18-29\ref*)
\p \v 11 We sailed from Troas straight to Samothrace, and the following day on to Neapolis. \v 12 From there \add we went\add* to \add the Roman\add* colony of Philippi, \add the\add* leading city of \add that\add* district of Macedonia.\f + \fr 16:12 \ft Literally \fqa we went to Philippi, which is a leading city of the district of Macedonia—a colony\f* And we stayed \add there several\add* days.
\p \v 13 On the Sabbath we went outside the city gate along \add the\add* river, where \add it\add* was customary to find \add a place of\add* prayer. \add After\add* sitting down, we spoke to the women who had gathered \add there\add*.
\p \v 14 Among those listening \add was\add* a woman named Lydia, a dealer in purple \add cloth\add* from \add the\add* city of Thyatira, \add who was\add* a worshiper of God. The Lord opened her heart to respond to Paul’s message. \v 15 And when \add she\add* and her household had been baptized, she urged \add us\add*, “If you consider me a believer in the Lord, come \add and\add* stay at my house.” And she persuaded us.
\s1 Paul and Silas Imprisoned
\p \v 16 One day as we were going to the \add place of\add* prayer, we were met by a slave girl with a spirit of divination,\f + \fr 16:16 \ft Greek \fqa a spirit of Python\ft ; that is, a spirit of divination named after the mythical serpent slain by Apollo\f* who earned a large income for her masters by fortune-telling. \v 17 \add This girl\add* followed Paul and \add the rest of\add* us, shouting, “These men are servants of the Most High God, who are proclaiming to you \add the\add* way of salvation!”
\p \v 18 She continued this for many days. \add Eventually\add* Paul grew so aggravated \add that\add* he turned \add and\add* said to the spirit, “In \add the\add* name of Jesus Christ I command you to come out of her!” And \add the spirit\add* left her at that very moment.
\p \v 19 \add When the girl’s\add* owners saw that their hope of making money was gone, they seized Paul and Silas \add and\add* dragged \add them\add* before the authorities in the marketplace. \v 20 They brought them to the magistrates \add and\add* said, “These men are Jews \add and\add* are throwing our city \add into turmoil\add* \v 21 \add by\add* promoting customs that are unlawful for us Romans to adopt \add or\add* practice.”
\p \v 22 The crowd joined in the attack against \add Paul and Silas\add*, and the magistrates ordered that they be stripped \add and\add* beaten with rods. \v 23 And after striking them with many blows, they threw \add them\add* into prison \add and\add* ordered the jailer to guard them securely. \v 24 On receiving \add this\add* order, he placed them in the inner cell and fastened their feet in the stocks.
\s1 The Conversion of the Jailer
\p \v 25 About midnight Paul and Silas were praying \add and\add* singing hymns to God, and the \add other\add* prisoners were listening to them. \v 26 Suddenly a strong earthquake shook the foundations of the prison. At once all the doors flew open and everyone’s chains came loose.
\p \v 27 \add When\add* the jailer woke up and saw the prison doors open, he drew his sword \add and\add* was about to kill himself, presuming that the prisoners had escaped. \v 28 But Paul called out in a loud voice, “Do not harm yourself! We are all here!”
\p \v 29 Calling for lights, \add the jailer\add* rushed in and fell trembling \add before\add* Paul and Silas. \v 30 Then he brought them out \add and\add* asked, “Sirs, what must I do to be saved?”
\p \v 31 They replied, “Believe in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved, you and your household.” \v 32 Then \add Paul and Silas\add* spoke the word of the Lord to him \add and\add* to everyone in his house. \v 33 At that hour of the night, \add the jailer\add* took them \add and\add* washed \add their\add* wounds. And without delay, he and all his household were baptized. \v 34 Then he brought them into \add his\add* home and set \add a meal before them\add*. \add So he\add* and all \add his\add* household rejoiced that they had come to believe in God.
\s1 An Official Apology
\p \v 35 \add When\add* daylight came, the magistrates sent \add their\add* officers with the order: “Release those men.”
\p \v 36 The jailer informed Paul: “The magistrates have sent orders to release you. Now you may go on your way in peace.”
\p \v 37 But Paul said to \add the officers\add*, “They beat us publicly without a trial \add and\add* threw \add us\add* into prison, \add even though\add* we are Roman citizens. And now do they want to send us away secretly? Absolutely not! Let them come themselves and escort us out!”
\p \v 38 \add So\add* the officers relayed this message to the magistrates, \add who\add* were alarmed to hear that \add Paul and Silas\add* were Roman citizens. \v 39 They came to appease them and led them out, requesting that they leave the city. \v 40 After \add Paul and Silas\add* came out of the prison, they went to Lydia’s house to see the brothers and encourage \add them\add*. Then they left \add the city \add*.
\c 17
\s1 The Uproar in Thessalonica
\p \v 1 \add When\add* they had passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Thessalonica, where there was a Jewish synagogue. \v 2 As was \add his\add* custom, Paul went into \add the synagogue\add*, and on three Sabbaths he reasoned with them from the Scriptures, \v 3 explaining and proving that the Christ had to suffer and rise from \add the\add* dead. “This Jesus I am proclaiming to you is the Christ,” \add he declared \add*. \v 4 Some of \add the Jews\add* were persuaded and joined Paul and Silas, along with a large number of God-fearing Greeks and \add quite\add* a few leading women.
\p \v 5 The Jews, however, became jealous. \add So\add* they brought in some troublemakers from the marketplace, formed a mob, \add and\add* sent the city into an uproar. They raided Jason’s house in search of \add Paul and Silas\add*, \add hoping\add* to bring them out to the people. \v 6 But when they could not find them, they dragged Jason and some other brothers before the city officials, shouting, “These \add men\add* who have turned the world \add upside down\add* have now come here, \v 7 \add and\add* Jason has welcomed them into his home. They \add are\add* all defying Caesar’s decrees, saying that there is another king, \add named \add* Jesus!”
\p \v 8 On hearing this, the crowd and city officials were greatly disturbed. \v 9 And they collected bond from Jason and the others \add and then\add* released them.
\s1 The Character of the Bereans
\p \v 10 As soon as night \add had fallen\add*, the brothers sent Paul and Silas \add away\add* to Berea. On arriving \add there\add*, they went into the Jewish synagogue. \v 11 Now \add the Bereans\add* were more noble-minded than the Thessalonians, \add for\add* they received the message with great eagerness \add and\add* examined the Scriptures every day \add to see\add* if these \add teachings\add* were true.\f + \fr 17:11 \ft Literally \fqa with great eagerness, every day examining the Writings, whether these things were so.\f* \v 12 As a result, many of them believed, along with \add quite\add* a few prominent Greek women and men.
\p \v 13 But when the Jews from Thessalonica learned that Paul was also proclaiming the word of God in Berea, they went there themselves to incite and agitate the crowds. \v 14 The brothers immediately sent Paul to the \add coast\add*, \add but\add* Silas and Timothy remained \add in Berea\add*. \v 15 Those who escorted Paul brought \add him\add* to Athens and then \add returned\add* with instructions for Silas and Timothy to \add join\add* him as soon as possible.
\s1 Paul in Athens
\p \v 16 \add While\add* Paul was waiting for them in Athens, he was deeply disturbed in his spirit to see that the city was full of idols. \v 17 So he reasoned in the synagogue with the Jews and God-fearing \add Gentiles\add*, and in the marketplace with those he met each day.
\p \v 18 Some Epicurean and Stoic philosophers also began to debate with him. Some \add of them\add* asked, “What \add is\add* this babbler trying to say?” Others \add said\add*, “He seems to be advocating foreign gods.” \add They said this\add* because \add Paul\add* was proclaiming the good news of Jesus and the resurrection.
\p \v 19 So they took \add Paul and\add* brought \add him\add* to the Areopagus,\f + \fr 17:19 \ft Or \fqa Ares Hill \ft or \fqa Mars Hill\ft ; also in verse 22 and added for clarity in verse 33\f* \add where\add* they asked \add him\add*, “May we know what this new teaching \add is that\add* you are presenting? \v 20 For you are bringing some strange notions to our ears, \add and\add* we want to know what \add they\add* mean.”
\p \v 21 Now all \add the\add* Athenians and foreigners who lived there spent their time \add doing\add* nothing \add more\add* than hearing and articulating new \add ideas\add*.
\s1 Paul’s Address in the Areopagus
\p \v 22 Then Paul stood up in \add the\add* meeting\f + \fr 17:22 \ft Literally \fqa in the middle\f* of the Areopagus \add and\add* said, “Men \add of\add* Athens, I see that in every way you \add are\add* very religious. \v 23 For as I walked around and examined your objects of worship, I even found an altar with this inscription:
\pc TO AN UNKNOWN GOD.
\p Therefore what you worship as something unknown, I \add now\add* proclaim to you.
\p \v 24 The God who made the world and everything in it is \add the\add* Lord of heaven and earth \add and does\add* not live in temples made by \add human\add* hands. \v 25 Nor is He served by human hands, as if He needed anything, \add because\add* He Himself gives everyone life and breath and everything \add else\add*. \v 26 From one \add man\add*\f + \fr 17:26 \ft Literally \fqa From one\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa From one blood\f* He made every nation of men, that they should inhabit the whole earth; and He determined \add their\add* appointed times and the boundaries of their lands.
\p \v 27  \add God intended that \add* they would seek \add Him and\add* perhaps reach out for Him and find \add Him\add*, though He is not far from each one of us. \v 28 ‘For in Him we live and move and have our being.’\f + \fr 17:28 \ft This is probably a quote from the Cretan philosopher Epimenides of Knossos.\f* As some of your \add own\add* poets have said, ‘We are \add His\add* offspring.’\f + \fr 17:28 \ft This is probably a quote from the poem “Phainomena” by the Cilician philosopher Aratus.\f* \v 29 Therefore, being offspring of God, we should not think that the Divine Being is like gold or silver or stone, an image formed by man’s skill and imagination.
\p \v 30 Although God overlooked the ignorance of \add earlier\add* times, He now commands all people everywhere to repent. \v 31 For He has set a day when He will judge the world with justice by the Man He has appointed. He has given proof \add of this\add* to everyone by raising Him from \add the\add* dead.”
\p \v 32 \add When\add* they heard about \add the\add* resurrection of \add the\add* dead, some began to mock \add him\add*, but \add others\add* said, “We want to hear you again on this \add topic\add*.” \v 33 At that, Paul left \add the Areopagus\add*. \v 34 But some\f + \fr 17:34 \ft Literally \fqa some men\f* joined him \add and\add* believed, including Dionysius the Areopagite, a woman named Damaris, and others \add who were\add* with them.
\c 18
\s1 Paul Ministers in Corinth
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 1:1–3|1CO 1:1-3\ref*; \ref 2 Corinthians 1:1–2|2CO 1:1-2\ref*)
\p \v 1 After this, \add Paul\add* left Athens \add and\add* went to Corinth. \v 2 \add There\add* he found a Jew named Aquila, a native of Pontus, who \add had\add* recently come from Italy with his wife Priscilla because Claudius had ordered all the Jews to leave Rome. \add Paul\add* went to \add visit \add* them, \v 3 and he stayed and worked with them because they were tentmakers by trade, \add just as\add* he was.
\p \v 4 Every Sabbath he reasoned in the synagogue, trying to persuade Jews and Greeks alike. \v 5 And when Silas and Timothy came down from Macedonia, Paul devoted himself fully to the word, testifying to the Jews \add that\add* Jesus is the Christ. \v 6 But \add when\add* they opposed and insulted \add him\add*, he shook out \add his\add* garments \add and\add* told them, “Your blood \add be\add* on your \add own\add* heads! I \add am\add* innocent \add of it\add*. From now on I will go to the Gentiles.”
\p \v 7 \add So Paul\add* left the synagogue \add and\add* went next door to \add the\add* house of Titus Justus,\f + \fr 18:7 \ft ECM; SBL, NE, and WH \fqa Titius Justus\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa Justus\f* a worshiper of God. \v 8 Crispus, the synagogue leader, and his whole household believed in the Lord. And many of the Corinthians who heard  \add the message \add* believed and were baptized.
\p \v 9 One night the Lord spoke to Paul in a vision: \wj “\add Do\add* not be afraid; keep on speaking; \add do\add* not be silent.\wj* \v 10 \wj For I am with you and no one will lay a hand on you, because I have many people in this city.”\wj* \v 11 \add So Paul\add* stayed for a year and \add a half\add*, teaching the word of God among \add the Corinthians\add*.
\s1 Paul before Gallio
\p \v 12 \add While\add* Gallio was proconsul of Achaia, the Jews coordinated an attack on Paul and brought him before the judgment seat. \v 13 “This \add man\add* is persuading the \add people\add* to worship God in ways contrary to the law,” they said.
\p \v 14 But \add just as\add* Paul was about to speak, Gallio told the Jews, “If this matter involved a wrongdoing or vicious crime, O Jews, it would be reasonable for me to hear your \add complaint \add*. \v 15 But since it is a dispute about words and names and your own law, settle \add it\add* yourselves. I refuse to be a judge of such things.” \v 16 And he drove them \add away\add* from the judgment seat.
\p \v 17 At this, the crowd\f + \fr 18:17 \ft Literally \fqa they all\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa all the Greeks\f* seized Sosthenes the synagogue leader \add and\add* beat \add him\add* in front of the judgment seat. \add But\add* none of this was of concern to Gallio.
\s1 Paul Returns to Antioch
\p \v 18 Paul remained \add in Corinth\add* for quite some time \add before\add* saying goodbye to the brothers. \add He had his\add* head shaved in Cenchrea to keep a vow he had \add made \add*, \add and then\add* he sailed for Syria, accompanied by Priscilla and Aquila.
\p \v 19 \add When\add* they reached Ephesus, \add Paul\add* left \add Priscilla and Aquila\add*. He himself went into the synagogue \add there and\add* reasoned with the Jews. \v 20 \add When\add* they asked \add him\add* to stay for a while longer, he declined. \v 21 But as he left, he said, “I will come back\f + \fr 18:21 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa I must by all means keep this feast that comes in Jerusalem, but I will come back\f* to you \add if\add* God is willing.” \add And\add* he set sail from Ephesus.
\p \v 22 \add When Paul\add* had landed at Caesarea, he went up and greeted the church \add at Jerusalem \add*.\f + \fr 18:22 \ft Literally \fqa he went up and greeted the church\f* \add Then\add* he went down to Antioch.
\s1 Paul’s Third Missionary Journey Begins
\r (\ref Acts 13:1–3|ACT 13:1-3\ref*; \ref Acts 15:36–41|ACT 15:36-41\ref*)
\p \v 23 \add After Paul\add* had spent some time \add in Antioch\add*, he traveled from place to place throughout the region of Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening all the disciples.
\p \v 24 Meanwhile a Jew named Apollos, a native of Alexandria, came to Ephesus. He was an eloquent man, well versed in the Scriptures. \v 25 He had been instructed in the way of the Lord and was fervent in spirit. He spoke and taught accurately about Jesus,\f + \fr 18:25 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa about the Lord\f* \add though\add* he knew only the baptism of John. \v 26 And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue. \add When\add* Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him aside and explained to him the way of God more accurately.
\p \v 27 \add When Apollos\add* resolved to cross over to Achaia, the brothers encouraged \add him and\add* wrote to the disciples \add there\add* to welcome him. On his arrival, he \add was a\add* great help to those who by grace had believed. \v 28 For he powerfully refuted the Jews in public \add debate\add*, proving from the Scriptures \add that\add* Jesus is the Christ.
\c 19
\s1 The Holy Spirit Received at Ephesus
\r (\ref Acts 10:44–48|ACT 10:44-48\ref*)
\p \v 1 While Apollos was at Corinth, Paul passed through the interior\f + \fr 19:1 \ft Or \fqa the highland\f* \add and\add* came to Ephesus. \add There\add* he found some disciples \v 2 and asked them, “Did you receive \add the\add* Holy Spirit when you became believers?”
\p “No,” \add they answered\add*, “we have not even heard that there is \add a\add* Holy Spirit.”
\p \v 3 “Into what, then, were you baptized?” \add Paul\add* asked.
\p “The baptism of John,” they replied.
\p \v 4 Paul explained: “John’s baptism was a baptism of repentance. He told the people to believe in the \add One\add* coming after him, that is, in Jesus.”
\p \v 5 On hearing this, they were baptized into the name of the Lord Jesus. \v 6 And \add when\add* Paul laid \add his\add* hands on them, the Holy Spirit came upon them, and they spoke in tongues and prophesied. \v 7 There were about twelve men in all.
\s1 Paul Ministers in Ephesus
\r (\ref Ephesians 1:1–2|EPH 1:1-2\ref*; \ref Revelation 2:1–7|REV 2:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 8 Then \add Paul\add* went into the synagogue \add and\add* spoke boldly \add there\add* for three months, arguing persuasively about the kingdom of God. \v 9 But when some of them stubbornly refused to believe \add and\add* publicly maligned the Way, \add Paul\add* took \add his\add* disciples \add and\add* left \add the synagogue\add* to conduct daily discussions in the lecture hall of Tyrannus. \v 10 This continued for two years, so that everyone who lived in \add the province of\add* Asia,\f + \fr 19:10 \ft Literally \fqa in Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey; similarly in verses 22, 26, 27, and 31.\f* Jews and Greeks alike, heard the word of the Lord.
\p \v 11 God did extraordinary miracles through the hands of Paul, \v 12 so that even handkerchiefs\f + \fr 19:12 \ft Greek \fqa soudaria\f* and aprons that had touched him were taken to the sick, and the diseases and evil spirits left them.
\s1 Seven Sons of Sceva
\p \v 13 Now \add there were\add* some itinerant Jewish exorcists who tried to invoke the name of the Lord Jesus over those with evil spirits. They would say, “I command you \add by\add* Jesus, whom Paul proclaims.” \v 14 Seven sons of Sceva, a Jewish chief priest, were doing this.
\p \v 15 But \add one day\add* the evil spirit responded, “Jesus I know, and I know about Paul, but who are you?” \v 16 Then the man with the evil spirit jumped on them \add and\add* overpowered them all. The attack was so violent that they ran out of the house naked and wounded.
\p \v 17 This became known to all the Jews and Greeks living in Ephesus, and fear came over all of them. \add So\add* the name of the Lord Jesus was held in high honor. \v 18 Many who had believed now came forward, confessing and disclosing their deeds. \v 19 And a number of those who had practiced magic arts brought \add their\add* books \add and\add* burned \add them\add* in front of everyone. \add When\add* the value of \add the books\add* was calculated, \add the total came to\add* fifty thousand drachmas.\f + \fr 19:19 \ft Or \fqa fifty thousand pieces of silver. \ft A drachma was a silver coin worth about one day’s wages.\f* \v 20 So the word of the Lord powerfully continued to spread and prevail.
\s1 The Riot in Ephesus
\p \v 21 After these things had happened, Paul resolved in the Spirit\f + \fr 19:21 \ft Or \fqa resolved in spirit\f* to go to Jerusalem, passing through Macedonia and Achaia. “After I have been there,” he said, “I must see Rome as well.” \v 22 He sent two of his helpers, Timothy and Erastus, to Macedonia, \add while\add* he stayed for a time in \add the province of\add* Asia.
\p \v 23 About that time there arose a great disturbance\f + \fr 19:23 \ft Literally \fqa there arose no little disturbance\f* about the Way. \v 24  \add It began with\add* a silversmith named Demetrius who made silver shrines of Artemis, bringing much business\f + \fr 19:24 \ft Literally \fqa bringing no little business\f* to the craftsmen.
\p \v 25 \add Demetrius\add* assembled \add the craftsmen\add*, along with the workmen in related trades. “Men,” he said, “you know that this business is our \add source of\add* prosperity. \v 26 And you can see and hear that not only in Ephesus, but in nearly the whole \add province of\add* Asia, this Paul has persuaded a great number of people to turn away. He says that man-made \add gods\add* are no gods \add at all\add*. \v 27 \add There is\add* danger not only \add that\add* our business will fall into disrepute, but also \add that\add* the temple of the great goddess Artemis will be discredited and her majesty deposed — \add she\add* who is worshiped by all \add the province of \add* Asia and the \add whole\add* world.”
\p \v 28 \add When the men\add* heard \add this\add*, they were enraged \add and\add* began shouting, “Great \add is\add* Artemis of \add the\add* Ephesians!” \v 29 \add Soon\add* the whole city was in disarray. They rushed together into the theatre, dragging with \add them\add* Gaius and Aristarchus, Paul’s traveling companions from Macedonia.
\p \v 30 Paul wanted to go before the assembly, \add but\add* the disciples would not allow him. \v 31 Even some of \add Paul’s\add* friends who were officials of \add the province of\add* Asia\f + \fr 19:31 \ft Or \fqa friends who were Asiarchs\f* sent word to him, begging him not to venture into the theatre.
\p \v 32 Meanwhile the assembly was in turmoil. Some were shouting one thing \add and some another\add*, and most \add of them\add* did not \add even\add* know why they were there. \v 33 The Jews in the crowd pushed Alexander \add forward\add* to explain himself, and \add he\add* motioned for silence so he could make \add his\add* defense to the people. \v 34 But when they realized that he was a Jew, they all shouted in unison for about two hours: “Great \add is\add* Artemis of \add the\add* Ephesians!”
\p \v 35 Finally the city clerk quieted the crowd \add and\add* declared, “Men \add of\add* Ephesus, doesn’t \add everyone\add* know that the city of Ephesus is guardian of the temple of the great Artemis and of \add her image\add*, which fell from heaven? \v 36 Since these things are undeniable, you ought to be calm and not do anything rash. \v 37 For you have brought these men \add here\add*, \add though they have\add* neither robbed \add our\add* temple nor blasphemed our goddess.
\p \v 38 So if Demetrius and his fellow craftsmen have a complaint against anyone, \add the\add* courts are open and proconsuls are available. Let them bring charges against one another \add there \add*. \v 39 But if you are seeking anything beyond this, it must be settled in a legal assembly. \v 40 For we are in jeopardy of being charged with rioting for today’s \add events\add*, \add and\add* we have no justification to account for this commotion.”
\p \v 41 \add After\add* he had said \add this\add*, he dismissed the assembly.
\c 20
\s1 Paul in Macedonia and Greece
\p \v 1 When the uproar had ended, Paul sent for the disciples. And after encouraging \add them\add*, he said goodbye \add to them and\add* left for Macedonia. \v 2 \add After\add* traveling through that area and speaking many words of encouragement, he arrived in Greece, \v 3 \add where\add* he stayed three months. \add And when\add* the Jews formed a plot against him \add as\add* he was about to sail for Syria, he decided to go back through Macedonia.
\p \v 4 \add Paul\add* was accompanied by Sopater \add son of\add* Pyrrhus from Berea, Aristarchus and Secundus from Thessalonica, Gaius from Derbe, Timothy, and Tychicus and Trophimus from \add the province of\add* Asia.\f + \fr 20:4 \ft Literally \fqa from Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey; also in verses 16 and 18.\f* \v 5 These \add men\add* went on ahead \add and\add* waited for us in Troas. \v 6 And after the \add Feast\add* of Unleavened Bread,\f + \fr 20:6 \ft Literally \fqa after the days of the Unleavened\ft ; see \ref Exodus 12:14–20|EXO 12:14-20\ref*.\f* we sailed from Philippi, and five days later we rejoined them in Troas, where we stayed seven days.
\s1 Eutychus Revived at Troas
\r (\ref 2 Kings 4:18–37|2KI 4:18-37\ref*)
\p \v 7 On the first \add day\add* of the week we came together to break bread. \add Since\add* Paul was ready to leave the next day, he talked to them and kept on speaking until midnight.
\p \v 8 Now there were many lamps in the upper room where we were gathered. \v 9 And a certain young man named Eutychus, seated in the window, was sinking into a deep sleep as Paul talked on and on. When he was sound asleep, he fell from the third story and was picked up dead. \v 10 But Paul went down, threw \add himself\add* on \add the young man\add*, and embraced \add him\add*. “\add Do\add* not be alarmed!” he said. “He is still alive!”
\p \v 11 Then \add Paul\add* went back upstairs, broke bread, and ate. And after speaking until daybreak, he departed. \v 12 And \add the people were\add* greatly relieved to take the boy \add home\add* alive.
\s1 From Troas to Miletus
\p \v 13 We went on ahead to the ship \add and\add* sailed to Assos, \add where\add* we were to take Paul \add aboard\add*. He had arranged this because he was going there on foot. \v 14 And when he met us at Assos, we took him aboard \add and\add* went on to Mitylene. \v 15 Sailing on from there, we arrived the next \add day\add* opposite Chios. The \add day\add* after that we arrived at Samos, and\f + \fr 20:15 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa and, after remaining at Trogyllium,\f* on the following \add day\add* we came to Miletus.
\p \v 16 Paul had decided to sail past Ephesus to avoid spending time in \add the province of\add* Asia, because he was in a hurry to reach Jerusalem, if possible, by the day of Pentecost.\f + \fr 20:16 \ft That is, Shavuot, the late spring feast of pilgrimage to Jerusalem; it is also known as \fqa the Feast of Harvest\ft (see \ref Exodus 23:16|EXO 23:16\ref*) or \fqa the Feast of Weeks\ft (see \ref Exodus 34:22|EXO 34:22\ref*).\f*
\s1 Paul’s Farewell to the Ephesians
\p \v 17 From Miletus, \add Paul\add* sent to Ephesus \add for\add* the elders of the church.
\p \v 18 When they came to him, he said, “You know how I lived the whole time \add I was\add* with you, from the first day I arrived in \add the province of\add* Asia. \v 19 I served the Lord with great humility and with tears, especially in the trials that came upon me through the plots of the Jews. \v 20 I did not shrink back from declaring \add anything\add* that was helpful to you \add as\add* I taught you publicly and from house to house, \v 21 testifying to Jews and Greeks alike \add about\add* repentance to God and faith in our Lord Jesus Christ.\f + \fr 20:21 \ft ECM, TR, and Tischendorf; SBL, NE, BYZ, and WH \fqa our Lord Jesus\f*
\p \v 22 And now, compelled by the Spirit, I am going to Jerusalem, not knowing what will happen to me \add there\add*. \v 23 \add I\add* only \add know\add* that in town after town the Holy Spirit warns me that chains and afflictions await me. \v 24 But I consider my life of no value to me, if only I may finish my course and  \add complete \add* the ministry I have received from the Lord Jesus — \add the ministry \add* of testifying to the good news of God’s grace.
\p \v 25 Now I know that \add none of\add* you among whom I have preached the kingdom will see my face \add again\add*. \v 26 Therefore I testify to you this day that I \add am\add* innocent of the blood of all \add men\add*.\f + \fr 20:26 \ft See \ref Ezekiel 33:8–9|EZK 33:8-9\ref*.\f* \v 27 For I did not shrink back from declaring to you the whole will of God.
\p \v 28 Keep watch over yourselves and the entire flock of which the Holy Spirit has made you overseers. Be shepherds of the church of God,\f + \fr 20:28 \ft Tischendorf \fqa of the Lord\ft ; BYZ and PT \fqa of the Lord and God\f* which He purchased with \add His\add* own blood.\f + \fr 20:28 \ft Or \fqa with the blood of His own Son.\f* \v 29 I know that after my departure, savage wolves will come in among you and will not spare the flock. \v 30 Even from your own number, men will rise up \add and\add* distort \add the truth\add* to draw away disciples after them. \v 31 Therefore be alert \add and\add* remember that for three years I never stopped warning each \add of you\add* night and day with tears.
\p \v 32 And now I commit you to God and to the word of His grace, which can build \add you\add* up and give you an inheritance among all who are sanctified.
\p \v 33 I have not coveted anyone’s silver or gold or clothing. \v 34 You yourselves know that these hands \add of mine\add* have ministered to my \add own\add* needs and \add those of\add* my companions. \v 35 In everything, I showed you that by this kind of hard work we must help the weak, remembering the words of the Lord Jesus Himself: \wj ‘It is more blessed to give than to receive.’\wj*”
\p \v 36 \add When Paul\add* had said this, he knelt down with all of them \add and\add* prayed. \v 37 They all wept openly \add as\add* they embraced Paul \add and\add* kissed him. \v 38 They were especially grieved by his statement that they would \add never\add* see his face \add again\add*. Then they accompanied him to the ship.
\c 21
\s1 Paul’s Journey to Jerusalem
\p \v 1 After we had torn ourselves away from them, we sailed directly to Cos, and the next \add day\add* on to Rhodes, and from there to Patara. \v 2 Finding a ship crossing over to Phoenicia, we boarded \add it and\add* set sail. \v 3 \add After\add* sighting Cyprus and passing south of it, we sailed on to Syria and landed at Tyre, \add where\add* the ship was to unload \add its\add* cargo.
\p \v 4 We sought out the disciples \add in Tyre and\add* stayed \add with them\add* seven days. Through the Spirit \add they\add* kept telling Paul not to go up to Jerusalem. \v 5 But when our time \add there\add* had ended, we set out on our journey. All \add the disciples\add*, with \add their\add* wives and children, accompanied us out of the city and knelt down on the beach to pray \add with us\add*. \v 6 \add And\add* after we had said our farewells, we went aboard the ship, and they returned home.
\p \v 7 \add When\add* we had finished \add our\add* voyage from Tyre, we landed at Ptolemais, \add where\add* we greeted the brothers \add and\add* stayed with them for a day.
\s1 Paul Visits Philip the Evangelist
\p \v 8 Leaving the next day, we went on to Caesarea and stayed at the home of Philip the evangelist, who was \add one\add* of the Seven.\f + \fr 21:8 \ft See \ref Acts 6:5|ACT 6:5\ref*.\f* \v 9 \add He\add* had four unmarried daughters who prophesied.
\p \v 10 After we had been there several days, a prophet named Agabus came down from Judea. \v 11 Coming over to us, he took Paul’s belt, bound his \add own\add* feet and hands, \add and\add* said, “The Holy Spirit says: ‘In this way the Jews of Jerusalem will bind the owner of this belt and hand \add him\add* over to \add the\add* Gentiles.’” \v 12 When we heard this, we and the \add people\add* there pleaded with \add Paul\add* not to go up to Jerusalem.
\p \v 13 Then Paul answered, “Why are you weeping and breaking my heart? I am ready not only to be bound, but also to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus.” \v 14 \add When\add* he would not be dissuaded, we quieted down \add and\add* said, “The Lord’s will be done.”
\p \v 15 After these days, we packed up \add and\add* went on to Jerusalem. \v 16 \add Some\add* of the disciples from Caesarea accompanied us, \add and\add* they took us to stay at the home of Mnason \add the\add* Cypriot, an early disciple.
\s1 Paul’s Arrival at Jerusalem
\p \v 17 When we arrived in Jerusalem, the brothers welcomed us joyfully. \v 18 The next \add day\add* Paul went in with us to \add see\add* James, and all the elders were present. \v 19 \add Paul\add* greeted them \add and\add* recounted one by one the things that God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry.
\p \v 20 When they heard \add this\add*, they glorified God. Then they said to \add Paul\add*, “You see, brother, how many thousands of Jews have believed, and all of them are zealous for the law. \v 21 But they are under the impression that you teach all \add the\add* Jews who \add live\add* among the Gentiles to forsake Moses, telling them not to circumcise \add their\add* children \add or\add* observe \add our\add* customs. \v 22 What then should \add we do\add*? They will certainly hear that you have come.
\p \v 23 Therefore do what we advise you. There are four men with us who have taken a vow. \v 24 Take these \add men\add*, purify yourself along with them, and pay their expenses so \add they can have their\add* heads shaved. Then everyone will know that there is no truth \add to these\add* rumors about you, but \add that\add* you also live in obedience to the law.
\p \v 25 As for the Gentile believers, we have written \add to them\add* our decision \add that\add* they must abstain from \add food\add* sacrificed to idols, \add from\add* blood, \add from\add* the meat of strangled animals, and \add from\add* sexual immorality.”
\p \v 26 \add So\add* the next day Paul took the men \add and\add* purified himself along with them. \add Then\add* he entered the temple to give notice of the date \add when their\add* purification would be complete \add and\add* the offering would be made for each of them.
\s1 Paul Seized at the Temple
\p \v 27 When the seven days were almost over, \add some\add* Jews from \add the province of\add* Asia\f + \fr 21:27 \ft Literally \fqa from Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* saw \add Paul\add* at the temple. They stirred up the whole crowd and seized him, \v 28 crying out, “Men \add of\add* Israel, help us! This is the man who teaches everyone everywhere against \add our\add* people and \add against our\add* law and \add against\add* this place. Furthermore, he has brought Greeks into the temple and defiled this holy place.” \v 29 For they had previously seen Trophimus the Ephesian with him in the city, \add and\add* they assumed that Paul had brought \add him\add* into the temple.
\p \v 30 The whole city was stirred up, and the people rushed together. They seized Paul \add and\add* dragged him out of the temple, and at once the gates were shut. \v 31 \add While\add* they were trying to kill him, the commander of the \add Roman\add* regiment\f + \fr 21:31 \ft Literally \fqa the commander of the cohort\f* received a report that all Jerusalem was in turmoil. \v 32 Immediately he took \add some\add* soldiers and centurions \add and\add* ran down to \add the crowd\add*. \add When the people\add* saw the commander and the soldiers, they stopped beating Paul.
\p \v 33 The commander came up \add and\add* arrested \add Paul\add*, ordering that he be bound with two chains. Then he asked who he was and what he had done.
\p \v 34 \add Some\add* in the crowd were shouting one thing, \add and some\add* another. And \add since the commander\add* could not get at the truth because of the uproar, he ordered that \add Paul\add* be brought into the barracks. \v 35 When \add Paul\add* reached the steps, he had to be carried by the soldiers because of the violence of the mob. \v 36 For the crowd that followed \add him\add* kept shouting, “Away with him!”
\s1 Paul Addresses the Crowd
\p \v 37 \add As\add* they were about to take Paul into the barracks, he asked the commander, “May I say something to you?”
\p “Do you speak Greek?” \add he\add* replied. \v 38 “Aren’t you the Egyptian who incited a rebellion some time ago and led four thousand members of the Assassins into the wilderness?”
\p \v 39 But Paul answered, “I am a Jew from Tarsus in Cilicia, a citizen \add of\add* no ordinary city. Now I beg you to allow me to speak to the people.”
\p \v 40 Having received permission, Paul stood on the steps \add and\add* motioned to the crowd. A great hush came over \add the crowd\add*, \add and\add* he addressed \add them\add* in Hebrew:\f + \fr 21:40 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\ft ; literally \fqa in the Hebrew language\f*
\c 22
\s1 Paul’s Defense to the Crowd
\r (\ref Acts 9:1–19|ACT 9:1-19\ref*; \ref Acts 26:1–23|ACT 26:1-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 “Brothers and fathers, listen now to my defense before you.” \v 2 \add When\add* they heard him speak to them in Hebrew,\f + \fr 22:2 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\ft ; literally \fqa in the Hebrew language\f* they became even more quiet.
\p Then \add Paul\add* declared, \v 3 “I am a Jew, born in Tarsus of Cilicia, but raised in this city. I was educated at the feet of Gamaliel in strict conformity to the law of our fathers. I was \add just as\add* zealous for God as any of you are today.
\p \v 4 \add I\add* persecuted this Way even to the death, detaining both men and women and throwing them into prison, \v 5 as the high priest and the whole Council can testify about me. I even obtained letters from them to \add their\add* brothers in Damascus, \add and\add* I was on my way to apprehend \add these people\add* and bring \add them\add* to Jerusalem to be punished.
\p \v 6 About noon \add as\add* I was approaching Damascus, suddenly a bright light from heaven flashed around me. \v 7 I fell to the ground and heard a voice say to me, \wj ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute Me?’\wj*
\p \v 8 ‘Who are You, Lord?’ I asked.
\p \wj ‘I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom you are persecuting,’\wj* He replied. \v 9 My companions saw the light, but they could not understand the voice of the \add One\add* speaking to me.
\p \v 10 Then I asked, ‘What should I do, Lord?’
\p \wj ‘Get up \add and\add* go into Damascus,’\wj* \add He\add* told me. \wj ‘There you will be told all that you have been appointed to do.’\wj*
\p \v 11 Because the brilliance of the light had blinded me, my companions led me by the hand into Damascus. \v 12 \add There\add* a man \add named\add* Ananias, a devout observer of the law \add who was\add* highly regarded by all the Jews living there, \v 13 came and stood beside me. ‘Brother Saul,’ he said, ‘receive your sight.’ And at that \add moment\add* I could see him.
\p \v 14 Then he said, ‘The God of our fathers has appointed you to know His will and to see the Righteous One and to hear \add His\add* voice. \v 15 You will be His witness to everyone of what you have seen and heard. \v 16 And now what are you waiting for? Get up, be baptized, and wash your sins \add away\add*, calling on His name.’
\p \v 17 Later, \add when\add* I had returned to Jerusalem and was praying at the temple, I fell into a trance \v 18 and saw \add the Lord\add*\f + \fr 22:18 \ft Literally \fqa saw Him\f* saying to me, \wj ‘Hurry! Leave Jerusalem quickly, because \add the people here\add* will not accept your testimony about Me.’\wj*
\p \v 19 ‘Lord,’ I answered, ‘they know very well that in one synagogue after another I imprisoned and beat those who believed in You. \v 20 And when the blood of Your witness\f + \fr 22:20 \ft Or \fqa martyr\f* Stephen was shed, I stood there giving my approval and watching over the garments of those who killed him.’
\p \v 21 Then He said to me, \wj ‘Go! I will send you far away to \add the\add* Gentiles.’\wj*”
\s1 Paul the Roman Citizen
\p \v 22 \add The crowd\add* listened to \add Paul\add* until he made this statement. Then they lifted up their voices \add and\add* shouted, “Rid the earth of \add him\add*! He is not fit to live!”
\p \v 23 \add As\add* they were shouting and throwing off \add their\add* cloaks and tossing dust into the air, \v 24 the commander ordered that \add Paul\add* be brought into the barracks. He directed that \add Paul\add* be flogged and interrogated to determine the reason for this outcry against him.
\p \v 25 But as they stretched him out to strap him down, Paul said to the centurion standing there, “Is it lawful for you to flog a Roman citizen without a trial?”
\p \v 26 \add On\add* hearing \add this\add*, the centurion went \add and\add* reported \add it\add* to the commander. “What are you going to do?” he said. “This man is a Roman citizen.”
\p \v 27 The commander went to \add Paul\add* and asked, “Tell me, are you a Roman citizen?”
\p “Yes,” he answered.
\p \v 28 “I paid a high price \add for my\add* citizenship,” said the commander.
\p “But I was born a citizen,” Paul replied.
\p \v 29 At once those who were about to interrogate \add Paul\add* stepped back, and the commander \add himself\add* was alarmed when he realized that he had put a Roman citizen \add in chains\add*.
\p \v 30 The next day \add the commander\add*, wanting to learn the real reason \add Paul\add* was accused by the Jews, released him and ordered the chief priests and the whole Sanhedrin\f + \fr 22:30 \ft Or \fqa the whole Council\f* to assemble. Then he brought Paul \add down\add* and had him stand before them.
\c 23
\s1 Paul before the Sanhedrin
\p \v 1 Paul looked directly at the Sanhedrin\f + \fr 23:1 \ft Or \fqa the Council\ft ; also in verses 6, 15, 20, and 28\f* \add and\add* said, “Brothers, I have conducted myself before God in all good conscience to this day.”
\p \v 2 At this, the high priest Ananias ordered those standing near \add Paul\add* to strike him on the mouth.
\p \v 3 Then Paul said to him, “God will strike \add you\add*, you whitewashed wall! You sit \add here\add* to judge me according to the law, yet you yourself violate the law by commanding that I be struck.”
\p \v 4 But those standing nearby said, “How dare you insult the high priest of God!”
\p \v 5 “Brothers,” Paul replied, “I was not aware that he was \add the\add* high priest, for it is written: ‘Do not speak evil \add about the\add* ruler of your people.’\f + \fr 23:5 \ref Exodus 22:28|EXO 22:28\ref* (see also LXX)\f*”
\p \v 6 Then Paul, knowing that some of them were Sadducees and the others Pharisees, called out in the Sanhedrin, “Brothers, I am a Pharisee, \add the\add* son of a Pharisee. \add It is\add* because of \add my\add* hope in the resurrection of \add the\add* dead \add that\add* I am on trial.”
\p \v 7 As soon as he had said \add this\add*, a dispute broke out between the Pharisees and Sadducees, and the assembly was divided. \v 8 For \add the\add* Sadducees say \add that\add* there is neither a resurrection nor angels nor spirits, but \add the\add* Pharisees acknowledge them all.
\p \v 9 A great clamor arose, and some scribes from the party of the Pharisees got up \add and\add* contended sharply, “We find nothing wrong with this man. What if a spirit or an angel has spoken to him?” \v 10 \add The\add* dispute grew so violent \add that\add* the commander was afraid they would tear Paul to pieces. He ordered the soldiers to go down \add and\add* remove him by force and bring \add him\add* into the barracks.
\p \v 11 The following night the Lord stood near \add Paul and\add* said, \wj “Take courage! As you have testified about Me in Jerusalem, so also you must testify in Rome.”\wj*
\s1 The Plot to Kill Paul
\r (\ref John 16:1–4|JHN 16:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 12 When daylight came, the Jews formed a conspiracy and bound themselves with an oath not to eat \add or\add* drink until they had killed Paul. \v 13 More than forty \add of them\add* were involved in this plot. \v 14 They went to the chief priests and elders \add and\add* said, “We have bound ourselves with a solemn oath \add not\add* to eat \add anything\add* until we have killed Paul. \v 15 Now then, you and the Sanhedrin petition the commander to bring him down to you on the pretext of examining his case more carefully. We are ready to kill him on the way.”
\p \v 16 But \add when\add* the son of Paul’s sister heard about the plot,\f + \fr 23:16 \ft Or \fqa the ambush\f* he went into the barracks \add and\add* told Paul. \v 17 Then Paul called one of the centurions \add and\add* said, “Take this young man to the commander; he has something to tell him.”
\p \v 18 So \add the centurion\add* took him to the commander and said, “Paul the prisoner sent \add and\add* asked me to bring this young man to you. He has something to tell you.”
\p \v 19 The commander took \add the young man\add* by the hand, drew \add him\add* aside, and asked, “What do you need to tell me?”
\p \v 20 He answered, “The Jews have agreed to ask you to bring Paul to the Sanhedrin tomorrow on the pretext of acquiring more information about him. \v 21 \add Do\add* not \add let\add* them persuade you, because more than forty men are waiting to ambush him. \add They\add* have bound themselves with an oath not to eat \add or\add* drink until they have killed him; they are ready now, awaiting your consent.”
\p \v 22 So the commander dismissed the young man and instructed \add him\add*, “Do not tell \add anyone\add* that you have reported this to me.”
\s1 Paul Sent to Felix
\p \v 23 Then he called two of \add his\add* centurions \add and\add* said, “Prepare two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen, and two hundred spearmen to go to Caesarea in the third hour of the night.\f + \fr 23:23 \ft That is, at nine tonight\f* \v 24 Provide mounts \add for\add* Paul to take \add him\add* safely to Governor Felix.” \v 25 \add And\add* he wrote the following letter:
\lf \v 26 Claudius Lysias,
\lf To His Excellency, Governor Felix:
\lf Greetings.
\lf \v 27 This man was seized by the Jews, and they were about to kill \add him\add* when I came with \add my\add* troops to rescue \add him\add*. For I had learned that he is a Roman citizen, \v 28 and since I wanted to understand \add their\add* charges \add against\add* him, I brought \add him\add* down to their Sanhedrin. \v 29 I found that the accusation involved questions \add about\add* their \add own\add* law, but there was no charge worthy of death or imprisonment.
\lf \v 30 \add When\add* I was informed that there was a plot against the man, I sent \add him\add* to you at once. I also instructed \add his\add* accusers to present \add their case\add* against him before you.
\p \v 31 So the soldiers followed their orders \add and\add* brought Paul by night to Antipatris. \v 32 The next day they returned to the barracks \add and\add* let the horsemen go on with him. \v 33 \add When the horsemen\add* arrived in Caesarea, they delivered the letter to the governor and presented Paul to him.
\p \v 34 \add The governor\add* read \add the letter\add* and asked what province \add Paul\add* was from. Learning that \add he was\add* from Cilicia, \v 35 he said, “I will hear your case when your accusers arrive.” Then he ordered that \add Paul\add* be kept under guard in Herod’s Praetorium.
\c 24
\s1 Tertullus Prosecutes Paul
\p \v 1 Five days later the high priest Ananias came down with some elders and a lawyer\f + \fr 24:1 \ft Or \fqa an orator\f* \add named\add* Tertullus, who presented to the governor \add their case\add* against Paul.
\p \v 2 \add When Paul\add* had been called in, Tertullus opened the prosecution: “Because of you, we have enjoyed a lasting peace, and your foresight has brought improvements to this nation. \v 3 In every way and everywhere, most excellent Felix, we acknowledge \add this\add* with all gratitude. \v 4 But in order not to delay you any further, I beg your indulgence to hear us briefly.
\p \v 5 We have found this man \add to be\add* a pestilence, stirring up dissension among the Jews all over the world. \add He is\add* a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes, \v 6 \add and he\add* even tried to desecrate the temple; \add so\add* we seized \add him\add*.\f + \fr 24:6 \ft TR includes \fqa and we would have judged him according to our law. \fv 7\fv*But Lysias the commander came with great force and took him out of our hands, \fv 8\fv*ordering his accusers to come before you.\f* \v 8 By examining him yourself, you will be able to learn \add the truth\add* about all our charges \add against\add* him.”
\p \v 9 The Jews concurred, asserting that these \add charges\add* were true.
\s1 Paul’s Defense to Felix
\p \v 10 \add When\add* the governor motioned for \add Paul\add* to speak, \add he\add* began his response: “Knowing that you have been a judge over this nation for many years, I gladly make my defense. \v 11 You can verify \add for yourself\add* that no more than twelve days ago I went up to Jerusalem to worship. \v 12 Yet \add my accusers\add* did not find me debating with anyone in the temple or riling up a crowd in the synagogues \add or\add* in the city. \v 13 Nor can they prove to you \add any of\add* their charges against me.
\p \v 14 I do confess to you, however, that I worship the God of our fathers according to the Way, which they call a sect. I believe everything that is laid down by the Law and written in the Prophets, \v 15 \add and\add* I have \add the same\add* hope in God that they themselves cherish, \add that\add* there will be a resurrection of both \add the\add* righteous and \add the\add* wicked. \v 16 In this \add hope\add*, I strive always to maintain a clear conscience before God and man.
\p \v 17 After several years, then, I returned  \add to Jerusalem \add* to bring alms to my people and to present offerings. \v 18 At the time they found me in the temple, I was ceremonially clean \add and\add* was not \add inciting a\add* crowd \add or\add* an uproar. But \add there are\add* some Jews from \add the province of\add* Asia\f + \fr 24:18 \ft Literally \fqa from Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* \v 19 who ought to appear before you and bring charges, if they have anything against me. \v 20 Otherwise, let \add these men\add* state for themselves any crime they found in me when I stood before the Sanhedrin,\f + \fr 24:20 \ft Or \fqa the Council\f* \v 21 unless \add it was\add* this one thing I called out as I stood in their \add presence\add*: ‘\add It is\add* concerning \add the\add* resurrection of \add the\add* dead \add that\add* I am on trial before you today.’”
\s1 The Verdict Postponed
\p \v 22 Then Felix, who was well informed about the Way, adjourned the hearing \add and\add* said, “When Lysias the commander comes, I will decide your case.” \v 23 He ordered the centurion to keep \add Paul\add* under guard, \add but\add* to allow him some freedom and permit his \add friends\add* to minister to his \add needs\add*.
\p \v 24 After several days, Felix returned with \add his\add* wife Drusilla, who was a Jewess. He sent for Paul and listened to him \add speak\add* about faith in Christ Jesus. \v 25 \add As Paul\add* expounded on righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment, Felix became frightened \add and\add* said, “You may go for now. \add When\add* I find the time, I will call for you.” \v 26 At the same time, he was hoping that Paul would offer him a bribe. So he sent for \add Paul\add* frequently \add and\add* talked with him.
\p \v 27 \add After\add* two years had passed, Felix was succeeded by Porcius Festus. And wishing to do the Jews a favor, Felix left Paul in prison.
\c 25
\s1 Paul’s Trial before Festus
\p \v 1 Three days after his arrival in the province, Festus went up from Caesarea to Jerusalem, \v 2 \add where\add* the chief priests and Jewish leaders presented their case against Paul. They urged \add Festus\add* \v 3 to grant them a concession against \add Paul\add* by summoning him to Jerusalem, \add because \add* they were preparing an ambush to kill him along the way.
\p \v 4 But Festus replied, “Paul is being held in Caesarea, and \add I myself\add* am going there soon. \v 5 So if \add this\add* man has done anything wrong, \add let\add* some of your leaders come down with me and accuse him \add there\add*.”
\p \v 6 \add After\add* spending no more than eight or ten days with them, \add Festus\add* went down to Caesarea. The next day he sat on the judgment seat \add and\add* ordered that Paul be brought in. \v 7 \add When Paul\add* arrived, the Jews who had come down from Jerusalem stood around him, bringing many serious charges that they could not prove.
\p \v 8 \add Then\add* Paul made his defense: “I have committed no offense against the law of the Jews \add or\add* against the temple \add or\add* against Caesar.”
\p \v 9 But Festus, wishing to do the Jews a favor, said to Paul, “Are you willing to go up to Jerusalem to stand trial before me on these \add charges\add*?”
\s1 Paul Appeals to Caesar
\p \v 10 Paul replied, “I am standing before the judgment seat of Caesar, where I ought to be tried. I have done nothing wrong to \add the\add* Jews, as you \add yourself\add* know very well. \v 11 If, however, I am guilty \add of\add* anything worthy of death, I do not refuse to die. But if there is no \add truth\add* to their accusations against me, no one has the right to hand me over to them. I appeal to Caesar!”
\p \v 12 Then Festus conferred with \add his\add* council \add and\add* replied, “You have appealed to Caesar. To Caesar you will go!”
\s1 Festus Consults Agrippa
\p \v 13 \add After\add* several days had passed, King Agrippa and Bernice came down to Caesarea to pay their respects to Festus. \v 14 Since they were staying several days, Festus laid out Paul’s case before the king: “There is a certain man \add whom\add* Felix left in prison. \v 15 While I was in Jerusalem, the chief priests and elders of the Jews presented their case \add and\add* requested a judgment against him. \v 16 I told \add them\add* that it is not \add the\add* Roman custom to hand a man \add over\add* before \add he\add* has had \add an\add* opportunity to face \add his\add* accusers and defend himself against \add their\add* charges.
\p \v 17 So \add when\add* they came here \add with me\add*, I did not delay. The next \add day\add* I sat on the judgment seat \add and\add* ordered that the man be brought in. \v 18 \add But when his\add* accusers rose to speak, they did not charge \add him\add* with \add any of the\add* crimes I had expected. \v 19 They only had some contentions with him regarding \add their\add* own religion and a certain Jesus \add who\add* had died, \add but\add* whom Paul affirmed to be alive.
\p \v 20 \add Since\add* I was at a loss as to how to investigate these \add matters\add*, I asked if he was willing to go to Jerusalem and be tried \add there\add* on these \add charges\add*. \v 21 But \add when\add* Paul appealed to be held over for the decision of the Emperor, I ordered that he be held until I could send him to Caesar.”
\p \v 22 Then Agrippa \add said\add* to Festus, “I would like to hear \add this\add* man myself.”
\p “Tomorrow you will hear him,” Festus declared.
\s1 Paul before Agrippa and Bernice
\p \v 23 The next day Agrippa and Bernice came with great pomp and entered the auditorium, along with \add the\add* commanders and leading men of the city. And Festus ordered that Paul be brought in.
\p \v 24 Then Festus said, “King Agrippa and all \add who\add* are present with us, you see this \add man\add*. The whole Jewish community has petitioned me about \add him\add*, both here and in Jerusalem, crying out \add that\add* he ought not to live \add any\add* longer. \v 25 But I found he had done nothing worthy of death, \add and\add* since he has now appealed to the Emperor, I decided to send \add him\add*.
\p \v 26 I have nothing definite to write to \add our\add* sovereign about \add him\add*. Therefore I have brought him before all of you, and especially before you, King Agrippa, so that after this inquiry I may have something to write. \v 27 For it seems unreasonable to me to send on a prisoner without specifying the charges against him.”
\c 26
\s1 Paul’s Testimony to Agrippa
\r (\ref Acts 9:1–19|ACT 9:1-19\ref*; \ref Acts 22:1–21|ACT 22:1-21\ref*)
\p \v 1 Agrippa said to Paul, “You have permission to speak for yourself.”
\p Then Paul stretched out \add his\add* hand \add and\add* began his defense: \v 2 “King Agrippa, I consider myself fortunate to stand before you today to defend myself against all the accusations of \add the\add* Jews, \v 3 especially \add since\add* you are acquainted with all the Jewish customs and controversies. I beg \add you\add*, therefore, to listen to me patiently.
\p \v 4 Surely all the Jews know how I have lived from \add my\add* earliest childhood among my own people, and also in Jerusalem. \v 5 They have known me for a long time \add and\add* can testify, if they are willing, that I lived as a Pharisee, adhering to the strictest sect of our religion.
\p \v 6 And now I stand on trial because of \add my\add* hope in the promise \add that\add* God made to our fathers, \v 7  \add the promise \add* our twelve tribes are hoping to see fulfilled as they earnestly serve \add God \add* day and night. \add It is\add* because of \add this\add* hope, O king, \add that\add* I am accused by the Jews. \v 8 Why \add would\add* any of you consider it incredible that God raises \add the\add* dead?
\p \v 9 So then, I \add too\add* was convinced that I ought to do all \add I could\add* to oppose the name of Jesus of Nazareth. \v 10 And \add that\add* is what I did in Jerusalem. With authority from the chief priests I put many of the saints in prison, and \add when\add* they were condemned to death, I cast my vote against \add them\add*. \v 11 I frequently had them punished in the synagogues, \add and\add* I tried to make \add them\add* blaspheme. In my raging fury against them, \add I\add* even \add went\add* to foreign cities to persecute \add them\add*.
\p \v 12 In this \add pursuit\add* I was on my way to Damascus with \add the\add* authority and commission of the chief priests. \v 13 About noon, O king, \add as I was\add* on the road, I saw a light from heaven, brighter than the sun, shining around me and my companions. \v 14 We all fell to the ground, \add and\add* I heard a voice say to me in Hebrew,\f + \fr 26:14 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\ft ; literally \fqa in the Hebrew language \f*\wj ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute Me? \add It is\add* hard for you to kick against \add the\add* goads.’\wj*
\p \v 15 ‘Who are You, Lord?’ I asked.
\p \wj ‘I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting,’\wj* the Lord replied. \v 16 \wj ‘But get up and stand on your feet. For I have appeared to you to appoint you as a servant and as a witness of what you have seen from Me and what I will show you.\wj* \v 17 \wj I will rescue you from \add your own\add* people and from the Gentiles. I am sending you to \add them\add*\wj* \v 18 \wj to open their eyes, so that they may turn from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, \add that\add* they may receive forgiveness of sins and \add an\add* inheritance among those sanctified by faith in Me.’\wj*
\p \v 19 So then, King Agrippa, I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision. \v 20 First to those in Damascus and Jerusalem, then to everyone in the region of Judea, and then to the Gentiles, I declared that they should repent and turn to God, performing deeds worthy \add of their\add* repentance. \v 21 For this reason the Jews seized me in the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 26:21 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\f* \add and\add* tried to kill \add me\add*.
\p \v 22 \add But\add* I have had God’s help to this day, \add and\add* I stand \add here\add* to testify to small and great alike. I am saying nothing beyond what the prophets and Moses said would happen: \v 23 that the Christ would suffer, \add and as the\add* first to rise from \add the\add* dead, would proclaim light to our people and to the Gentiles.”
\s1 Festus Interrupts Paul’s Defense
\p \v 24 At this stage of \add Paul’s\add* defense, Festus exclaimed in a loud voice, “You are insane, Paul! \add Your\add* great learning is driving you to madness!”
\p \v 25 But Paul answered, “I am not insane, most excellent Festus; I am speaking words of truth and sobriety. \v 26 For the king knows about these \add matters\add*, and I can speak freely to \add him\add*. I am confident that none of this has escaped his \add notice\add*, because \add it\add* was not done in a corner. \v 27 King Agrippa, do you believe the prophets? I know you \add do\add*.”
\p \v 28 Then Agrippa \add said\add* to Paul, “Can you persuade me in such a short \add time\add* to become a Christian?”
\p \v 29 “Short \add time or\add* long,” Paul \add replied\add*, “I wish to God that not only you but all who hear me this day may become what I am, except for these chains.”
\p \v 30 Then the king and the governor rose, along with Bernice and those seated with them. \v 31 On their way out, they said to one another, “This man has done nothing worthy of death or imprisonment.”
\p \v 32 And Agrippa said to Festus, “This man could have been released if he had not appealed to Caesar.”
\c 27
\s1 Paul Sails for Rome
\p \v 1 When it was decided \add that\add* we would sail for Italy, Paul and some other prisoners were handed over to a centurion named Julius, \add who belonged to the\add* Imperial\f + \fr 27:1 \ft Or \fqa Augustan\f* Regiment. \v 2 We boarded an Adramyttian ship about to sail for ports along \add the coast of\add* Asia,\f + \fr 27:2 \ft Literally \fqa sail to the places along Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* \add and\add* we put out to sea. Aristarchus, a Macedonian from Thessalonica, was with us.
\p \v 3 The next \add day\add* we landed at Sidon, and Julius treated Paul with consideration, allowing \add him\add* to visit his friends \add and\add* receive \add their\add* care. \v 4 \add After\add* putting out from there, we sailed to the lee of Cyprus because the winds were against us. \v 5 And \add when\add* we had sailed across the open sea off the coast of Cilicia and Pamphylia, we came to Myra in Lycia. \v 6 There the centurion found an Alexandrian ship sailing for Italy \add and\add* put us on \add board\add*.
\p \v 7 \add After\add* sailing slowly for many days, we arrived off Cnidus. \add When\add* the wind impeded us, we sailed to the lee of Crete, opposite Salmone. \v 8 \add After\add* we had moved along the coast with difficulty, we came to a place called Fair Havens, near \add the\add* town of Lasea.
\p \v 9 By now much time had passed, and the voyage had already become dangerous because it was after the Fast.\f + \fr 27:9 \ft That is, Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement\f* \add So\add* Paul advised \add them\add*, \v 10 “Men, I can see that \add our\add* voyage will be \add filled\add* with disaster and great loss, not only to ship and cargo, but to our \add own\add* lives as well.”
\p \v 11 But contrary to Paul’s advice, the centurion was persuaded by the pilot and by the owner of the ship. \v 12 Since the harbor was unsuitable to winter in, the majority decided to sail on, hoping that somehow they could reach Phoenix to winter \add there\add*. \add Phoenix was\add* a harbor in Crete facing \add both\add* southwest and northwest.
\s1 The Storm at Sea
\r (\ref Jonah 1:4–10|JON 1:4-10\ref*)
\p \v 13 \add When\add* a \add gentle\add* south wind began to blow, they thought they had \add their\add* opportunity. \add So\add* they weighed \add anchor and\add* sailed along, hugging \add the coast\add* of Crete. \v 14 But \add it was\add* not long \add before\add* a cyclone called the Northeaster swept down across \add the island\add*. \v 15 Unable to head into the wind, the ship was caught up. \add So\add* we gave way \add and\add* let ourselves be driven along.
\p \v 16 Passing to the lee of a small island called Cauda,\f + \fr 27:16 \ft NE, BYZ, and TR \fqa Clauda\f* we barely managed to secure the lifeboat. \v 17 After hoisting it up, \add the crew\add* used ropes to undergird the ship. And fearing that they would run aground on the sandbars of Syrtis, they lowered the sea anchor\f + \fr 27:17 \ft Or \fqa the sails\f* \add and\add* were driven along.
\p \v 18 We were tossed so violently \add that\add* the next \add day the men\add* began to jettison the cargo. \v 19 On the third \add day\add*, they threw the ship’s tackle \add overboard\add* with \add their\add* own hands. \v 20 \add When\add* neither sun nor stars appeared for many days and \add the great\add* storm continued to batter us, we abandoned all hope of being saved.
\p \v 21 \add After the men\add* had gone a long time without food, Paul stood up among them \add and\add* said, “Men, you should have followed my advice not to sail from Crete. Then \add you would have averted\add* this disaster and loss. \v 22 \add But\add* now I urge you to keep up your courage, because you will not experience \add any\add* loss of life, but only of the ship. \v 23 For \add just last\add* night an angel of the God to whom I belong and whom I serve stood beside me \v 24 \add and\add* said, ‘\add Do\add* not be afraid, Paul; you must stand before Caesar. And look, God has granted you \add the lives of\add* all who sail with you.’
\p \v 25 So take courage, men, for I believe God that it will happen just as He told me. \v 26 However, we must run aground on some island.”
\s1 The Shipwreck
\p \v 27 \add On\add* the fourteenth night we were still being driven across the Adriatic Sea.\f + \fr 27:27 \ft The Adriatic Sea referred to an area extending well south of Italy to include the central portion of the Mediterranean Sea.\f* About midnight the sailors sensed they were approaching land. \v 28 They took soundings \add and\add* found that \add the water was\add* twenty fathoms \add deep\add*.\f + \fr 27:28 \fqa 20 fathoms \ft is approximately 120 feet or 36.6 meters.\f* Going a little farther, they took another set of soundings that read fifteen fathoms.\f + \fr 27:28 \fqa 15 fathoms \ft is approximately 90 feet or 27.4 meters.\f* \v 29 Fearing that we would run aground on \add the\add* rocks, they dropped four anchors from \add the\add* stern \add and\add* prayed for daybreak.
\p \v 30 Meanwhile, the sailors attempted to escape from the ship. Pretending to lower anchors from \add the\add* bow, they let the lifeboat \add down\add* into the sea. \v 31 \add But\add* Paul said to the centurion and the soldiers, “Unless these \add men\add* remain with the ship, you cannot be saved.” \v 32 \add So\add* the soldiers cut the ropes to the lifeboat and set it adrift.
\p \v 33 Right up to daybreak, Paul kept urging them all to eat: “Today \add is your\add* fourteenth day in constant suspense, without taking any food. \v 34 So for your \add own\add* preservation, I urge you to eat something, because not a single hair of your head will be lost.”
\p \v 35 \add After\add* he had said this, \add Paul\add* took bread \add and\add* gave thanks to God in front of them all. Then he broke \add it and\add* began to eat. \v 36 They were all encouraged and took \add some\add* food themselves. \v 37 In all, there were 276 \f + \fr 27:37 \ft WH \fqa 76\f* of us on \add board\add*. \v 38 \add After the men\add* had eaten their fill, they lightened the ship by throwing the grain into the sea.
\p \v 39 When daylight came, they did not recognize the land, but they sighted a bay with a sandy beach, where they decided to run the ship \add aground\add* if they could. \v 40 Cutting away the anchors, they left \add them\add* in the sea as they loosened the ropes \add that held\add* the rudders. Then they hoisted the foresail to the \add wind and\add* made for the beach. \v 41 But the ship struck a sandbar \add and\add* ran aground. The bow stuck fast \add and\add* would not move, and the stern was being broken up by the pounding of the waves.
\p \v 42 The soldiers planned to kill the prisoners so none of them could swim to freedom. \v 43 But the centurion, wanting to spare Paul’s  \add life \add*, thwarted their plan. He commanded those who could swim to jump overboard first \add and\add* get to land. \v 44 The rest \add were to follow\add* on planks and various parts of the ship. In this way everyone was brought safely to land.
\c 28
\s1 Ashore on Malta
\p \v 1 \add Once\add* we were safely ashore, we learned that the island was called Malta. \v 2 The islanders showed us extraordinary kindness. They kindled a fire \add and\add* welcomed all of us because \add it was\add* raining and cold.
\p \v 3 Paul gathered a bundle of sticks, and as he laid \add them\add* on the fire, a viper, driven out by the heat, fastened \add itself\add* to his hand. \v 4 When the islanders saw the creature hanging from his hand, they said to one another, “Surely this man is a murderer. \add Although he\add* was saved from the sea, Justice\f + \fr 28:4 \ft Greek \fqa Dike\ft ; that is, the Greek goddess of justice\f* \add has\add* not allowed \add him\add* to live.” \v 5 But \add Paul\add* shook the creature \add off\add* into the fire \add and\add* suffered no ill effects. \v 6 \add The islanders\add* were expecting him to swell up or suddenly drop dead. But after waiting a long time and seeing nothing unusual happen to him, they changed their minds \add and\add* said he was a god.
\p \v 7 Nearby stood an estate belonging to Publius, \add the\add* chief official of the island. \add He\add* welcomed us \add and\add* entertained \add us\add* hospitably \add for\add* three days. \v 8 The father of Publius was sick in bed, suffering from fever and dysentery. Paul went in to \add see him\add*, and after praying \add and\add* placing \add his\add* hands on him, he healed \add the man\add*. \v 9 \add After\add* this had happened, the rest of the sick on the island came and were cured as well.
\p \v 10 \add The islanders\add* honored us in many ways and supplied \add our\add* needs \add when we were ready\add* to sail.
\s1 Paul Arrives in Italy
\p \v 11 After three months we set sail in an Alexandrian ship that had wintered in the island. It had the Twin Brothers\f + \fr 28:11 \ft Greek \fqa Dioscuri\ft ; that is, the Greek gods Castor and Pollux\f* as a figurehead. \v 12 Putting in at Syracuse, we stayed there three days. \v 13 From \add there\add* we weighed anchor and came to Rhegium. After one day, a south wind came up, \add and\add* on the second day we arrived at Puteoli. \v 14 \add There\add* we found \add some\add* brothers who invited us to spend \add the\add* week with them. And so we came to Rome.
\p \v 15 The brothers there had heard about us \add and\add* traveled as far as \add the\add* Forum of Appius\f + \fr 28:15 \ft The Forum of Appius was about 43 miles or 70 kilometers from Rome.\f* and \add the\add* Three Taverns\f + \fr 28:15 \ft The Three Taverns was about 35 miles or 57 kilometers from Rome.\f* to meet us. \add When\add* Paul saw \add them\add*, he was encouraged and gave thanks to God.
\s1 Paul Preaches at Rome
\r (\ref Isaiah 6:1–13|ISA 6:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 16 When we arrived in Rome,\f + \fr 28:16 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa the centurion delivered up the prisoners to the captain of the barrack, but\ft .\f* Paul was permitted to stay by himself, with a soldier to guard him.
\p \v 17 After three days, he called together the leaders of the Jews. \add When\add* they had gathered, he said to them, “Brothers, \add although\add* I have done nothing against \add our\add* people or the customs of our fathers, I was taken prisoner in Jerusalem \add and\add* handed over to the Romans. \v 18 \add They\add* examined me \add and\add* wanted to release \add me\add*, because there was no basis for a death sentence against me. \v 19 But \add when\add* the Jews objected, I was compelled to appeal to Caesar, \add even though\add* I have no \add charge\add* to bring against my nation. \v 20 So for this reason I have called to see you and speak with \add you\add*. \add It is\add* because of the hope of Israel \add that\add* I am bound with this chain.”
\p \v 21 \add The leaders\add* replied, “We have not received \add any\add* letters about you from Judea, nor \add have\add* any of the brothers \add from there\add* reported or even mentioned anything bad about you. \v 22 But we consider your views \add worth\add* hearing, because we know that \add people\add* everywhere are speaking against this sect.”
\p \v 23 So they set a day to \add meet with Paul\add*, \add and\add* many \add people\add* came to \add the place\add* he was staying. He expounded to \add them\add* from morning to evening, testifying about the kingdom of God and persuading them about Jesus from the Law of Moses and the Prophets.
\p \v 24 Some \add of them\add* were convinced by what he said, but \add others\add* refused to believe. \v 25 They disagreed among \add themselves and\add* began to leave \add after\add* Paul had made \add this final\add* statement: “The Holy Spirit was right when He spoke to your fathers through Isaiah the prophet:
\b 
\q1 \v 26 ‘Go to this people and say,
\q2 “You will be ever hearing \add but\add* never understanding;
\q2 you will be ever seeing \add but\add* never perceiving.”
\q1 \v 27 For this people’s heart has grown callous;
\q2 they hardly hear with \add their\add* ears,
\q2 and they have closed their eyes.
\q1 Otherwise they might see with \add their\add* eyes,
\q2 hear with \add their\add* ears,
\q2 understand with \add their\add* hearts,
\q1 and turn, and I would heal them.’\f + \fr 28:27 \ref Isaiah 6:9–10|ISA 6:9-10\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 28 Be advised, therefore, that God’s salvation has been sent to the Gentiles, and they will listen!”\f + \fr 28:28 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 29\fv*When he had said this, the Jews went away, disputing sharply among themselves.\f*
\p \v 30 \add Paul\add* stayed there two full years in his own rented house, welcoming all who came to \add visit\add* him. \v 31 Boldly \add and\add* freely he proclaimed the kingdom of God and taught about the Lord Jesus Christ.
